\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The strength of the Mexican peso\b0\par\par\uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 After a brief period of weakening triggered by the financial turbulence in the United States in early 2023, the Mexican peso has resumed an appreciating trend. During the course of this year alone, our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strengthened by more than 12% against the US dollar. This has generated concern in some sectors for its potentially harmful effects on the competitiveness of our economy, as well as fears that the peso's appreciation may continue for a prolonged period of time.\par \par \uc2 My impression, I believe, like that of most private sector analysts, given the trajectory they project for the peso's parity against the dollar in the coming months, is that the trend of appreciation of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not last long. Why?\par \par \uc2 The strength of the peso has several determinants. I believe that three stand out among them. The first is the combination of a high level of interest rates in Mexico and a moderate volatility of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate, which makes domestic financial instruments more attractive than those observed in other emerging economies. The above encourages a strong demand for peso-denominated assets, albeit of a very short-term and highly volatile nature.\par \par \uc2 Remittances from Mexican workers abroad represent another important source of pressure on the peso. The growth of these inflows over the last few years has been formidable. From 2018 to 2022 remittances increased by more than 70%, reaching in the last of these years just over 4% of GDP. What explains this evolution? The strong demand for labor in the United States, driven by measures to deal with the ravages caused by the pandemic and the war in Ukraine, but also the inability of the Mexican economy to retain these workers.\par \par \uc2 The third determinant of the peso's recent appreciation that I wish to highlight is Mexico's macroeconomic position. In particular, a small balance of payments current account deficit, a comfortable international reserve position, prudent monetary policy and reasonable public debt to GDP ratios, which together place the Mexican economy in a favorable position in the emerging economy environment.\par \par \uc2 I would like to note that, contrary to what is often argued, the latter is far from being indicative of sound economic fundamentals. The strength of the current account of the balance of payments is mainly the result of high remittance inflows, i.e., of deficient public policies that have prevented a significant part of our country's human capital from being tapped. On the other hand, while public debt is moderate today, government finances are characterized by very low revenues and spending rigidities that make them worryingly fragile over time.\par \par \uc2 What information can be drawn for the future trajectory of the peso from the outlook for these determinants?\par \par \uc2 The interest rate differential favorable to Mexico is likely to narrow in the coming months. As inflation declines, the ex ante real policy rate (i.e., the nominal interest rate minus expected inflation) will tend to increase. At a time when economic activity is also expected to weaken, this opens up the possibility of nominal interest rate reductions. The interest rate differential may also be diminished by its external component, as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  (FED) has suggested that its interest rate may show one or two additional interest rate increases before the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, remittance inflows to Mexico are likely to weaken. The U.S. labor market remains strong. However, it is difficult to believe that the sharp adjustment in U.S. interest rates will not have an impact on U.S. economic activity. In fact, in its most recent projections, the FED anticipates GDP growth rates of only 1 and 1.1% for 2023 and 2024, respectively, and the possibility of a recession remains latent.\par \par \uc2 To the above, we should add the risks derived from the important electoral process in Mexico next year. In addition to the nervousness that this may cause in financial markets, the peso could be affected if there is a relaxation of macroeconomic policy, aimed at counteracting the possible weakening of the economy and the consequent political costs.\par \par \uc2 Nor should we forget that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Commission, made up of representatives of \uc2 Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 M\u233\'00\'E9xico\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has the tools to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, without affecting the floating regime, if the exchange rate reaches levels considered inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I believe it is important to point out that, although it is important to carefully monitor the implications of the trajectory of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we must keep in mind the enormous benefits that the flexible exchange rate regime has brought us, and be aware that in the search for a competitive position in the markets, exchange rate policy cannot act as a substitute for improvements in productivity.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230629ej6s00231\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
