\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2023\par 22:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1641 ET - The IPC stock index closes up 0.2% at 55033 points, with financial group Banorte shares up 1.5% and conglomerate \uc2 Femsa\uc2  advancing 1%. The peso slips to 17.80 to the US dollar from 17.76 Friday. Last week the peso ended at its strongest level since September 2017. DBRS Morningstar affirmed Mexico's sovereign rating at BBB, saying government commitment to fiscal sustainability "should help preserve stable macroeconomic conditions, even as the global environment becomes more challenging." Kroll Bond Ratings Agency also ratified Mexico at BBB, the Finance Ministry says, adding the government seeks to maintain favorable access to domestic and international markets through solid handling of public finances. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1602 ET - The banking turmoil "doesn't appear to have triggered a severe further tightening in credit conditions," Capital Economics' \uc2 Paul Ashworth\uc2  ways in a report about the Fed's April Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey. "Although there was only a modest post-SVB additional tightening in lending standards on [commercial real state and commercial and industrial] lending, banks do appear to be more concerned about household lending," Ashworth says. "Higher interest rates are now weighing heavily on the demand for credit." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1551 ET - Treasury yields rise as markets digest Friday's surprisingly strong labor data ahead of inflation data due later this week. The 10-year gains 0.073 percentage point to 3.518% and the two-year rises 0.089 pp to 4.009%. The benchmarks weakened after the Fed indicated last week it could keep rates unchanged in June. April payrolls, however, raised the prospect of another rate increase, fueling a Treasurys selloff, while the risk of bank failures and the debt-ceiling standoff could still trigger a run for safety. CPI inflation Wednesday is expected to show prices still resilient. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1534 ET - The Brazilian government nominated two new members to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee, including one who is close to the government of President \uc2 Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva\uc2 , which could generate some friction within the committee, said \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Alberto Ramos in a research note. Da Silva has called for the bank to cut interest rates to boost economic growth. Gabriel Galipolo, one of the two men nominated, is currently an official in the Finance Ministry. Galipolo is also seen as a possible replacement for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current president, according to Ramos. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1534 ET - Brazil's motor vehicle production fell in April from a year earlier amid weak demand, according to the \uc2 Brazilian Association of Automotive Vehicle Manufacturers\uc2 , or \uc2 Anfavea\uc2 . Production declined 3.9% from the same month a year earlier and dropped 19.4% from March, the group said. But even with high interest rates in the country slowing sales, 14% more vehicles have been sold in the year through April than in the same period of 2022, \uc2 Anfavea\uc2  President Marcio de Lima Leite said. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1528 ET - The US labor market is gradually softening, but not fast enough to support market bets on Fed cuts this year, \uc2 Apollo\uc2 's \uc2 Torsten Slok\uc2  says. He notes that total nonfarm payrolls are below their six-month moving average, while saying that inflation "is sticky because of continued high wage inflation and the housing market recovering." Slok adds that "the market is underestimating the Fed's commitment to get inflation down from currently 5% to the FOMC's 2% inflation target." Markets are pricing rate cuts starting in September. "I don't think the Fed will cut rates in 2023," Slok says. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1525 ET - Brazilian exports of pork rose in April from a year earlier as some Asian countries battled outbreaks of African swine fever, said the \uc2 Brazilian Animal Protein Association\uc2 , or ABPA. Exports rose 16.6% to 104,500 tons of the meat last month, while export revenue jumped 29.9% to $251.3M in the period, the ABPA said. China continued to be the biggest importer of Brazilian pork in this year through April, buying a total of 143,200 tons in the period, according to the ABPA. Hong Kong was another big buyer in the first four months of this year, with imports of 42,200 tons, ABPA said. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1430 ET - Rising social tension increases credit risk for Latin American issuers "via governability; policy shifts, including regulatory changes and government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; economic performance and financial volatility channels," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  researchers say in a report. They point to financial volatility a risk in Argentina, while policy shifts in Brazil "may lead to government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state-owned enterprises." Changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's energy policy "have dampened private investment," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says, and social protest in Peru is a potential threat to the country's sovereign, banking, tourism and retail issuers. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1302 ET - The market's pricing of a pivot to Fed cuts is out of sync with the FOMC's commitment to bringing down inflation, say \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. But if the hiking cycle is indeed at an end, they say it shouldn't imply dollar weakness. "A common thread to past pause periods has indeed been dollar depreciation at the onset, but the direction is less clear over the full duration of the rate hold," \uc2 BofA\uc2  says. The strategists say the past three periods where the Fed paused rate hikes produced differing results for the USD, and they expect the dollar to find near-term support. "Inflation has remained sticky and should keep the Fed committed to holding rates at terminal into 2024, well beyond market pricing," they say. The ICE US dollar index is little changed. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1259 ET - Retailers are signaling that a slowdown in imports is deepening. \uc2 The National Retail Federation\uc2 's Global Port Tracker forecasts imports at the nation's busiest container ports in April and May will be 23% to 24% lower than the same time last year. Ben Hackett, of Hackett Associates which produces the report, says his group is revising down forecasts as economic uncertainty weighs on retailers. Import volumes during 3Q peak shipping season are expected to be 7.2% below last year's levels. "Our view is that imports will remain below recent levels until inflation rates and inventory surpluses are reduced," Hackett says. (paul.berger@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1203 ET - Retail gasoline prices swung sharply in both directions last month, adding some uncertainty as to what the \uc2 BLS\uc2 's overall US Consumer Price Index for April will reveal Wednesday morning. Volatile fuel prices are often a key factor in the overall CPI number that's expected to be up 0.4% month-over-month. Gasoline prices surged by 5% during the first few weeks of April after \uc2 OPEC\uc2 -plus's surprise production-cut announcement, and reached a 2023 high of $3.68/gallon April 23, according to AAA. But recession fears and other issues caused crude-oil prices to fall in late April, and fuel prices at the pump quickly followed with steep declines of their own, leaving gasoline prices to finish April not much higher from where they began. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1200 ET - Risks pressuring investment-grade spreads are likely to dissipate next month, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 's Yuri Seliger says in a report. "We look for IG spreads to remain biased wider in May due to a combination of three risks: 1) heavy supply, 2) a resurgence of bank stress and 3) the upcoming US debt limit x-date." Seliger says issuance seasonally slows in the summer, while "more elevated bank stress increases the likelihood of a solution." Similarly, the approaching of an X-date in which the government won't be able to fully pay its bills increases the possibility of a compromise in Congress, he says. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 08, 2023 16:41 ET (20:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020230508ej58003gi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
