\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 World  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Map: Mexican peso, among the oldest  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  in the Americas\b0\par\par\uc2 1,387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2023\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso is the official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico, and one of the oldest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America, since it began to be used in the XVI century, after the arrival of the Spaniards to the continent, with a wide variety of bills and coins, the \uc2 Bank of Mexico (Banxico) \uc2 is in charge of the issuance and distribution of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .It has bills of 20, 50, 100, 200, 500 and 1000 Mexican pesos, as well as coins of 50 cents and one, two, five, 10 and some commemorative coins of 20 pesos.In World Coin Map we will share with you the essential data of the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the global market, we have already written about the Euro(?) and the US dollar ($), and we will continue with the Mexican peso.History of the Mexican pesoAccording to the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 , the history of the peso dates back to the 16th century, after the arrival of the Spaniards to the country, when the colonizers introduced the royal coin of eight, which was the silver coin used in Europe and ended up becoming the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of New Spain.After Mexico's independence in 1821, it was established as a federal republic and it is right at that moment that the first official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  called Mexican peso was created, which was based on the value of the old real de ocho.The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  assures that, during the 19th century, the Mexican peso suffered multiple devaluations due to the economic and political instability \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  was going through.In 1864, during the French \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a paper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  called "Peso Fuerte" was issued, whose value was greater than that of the Mexican peso. However, after the restoration of the republic, the use of paper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was discontinued.Creation of the Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xicoYears later, in 1925, the \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2  was created, a central body whose main objective continues to be the regularization of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issuance and monetary policy.Along with the creation of the also called \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , the gold standard was introduced, which meant that the peso could be exchanged for gold at a fixed value.Today, the Mexican peso remains Mexico's official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is widely used in trade and tourism, and the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  remains in charge of regulating the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issuance and monetary policy.The peso vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis the worldThe Mexican peso is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is considered stable and relatively liquid in international financial markets.However, in times of global economic uncertainty or volatility, as occurred during the 2008 financial crisis or the covid-19 pandemic, the Mexican peso can be affected by capital flight and devaluation against other strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the U.S. dollar.In addition, domestic factors such as inflation, insecurity, corruption and political uncertainty can influence the perception of the Mexican peso.In general, macroeconomic stability and fiscal policies are considered fundamental to maintain confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strengthen its position in international markets.Elements of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencyIn\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Political Constitution of the United Mexican States, it establishes among its paragraphs the functions of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2 , and it is exclusively responsible for minting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and issuing banknotes.Mexico's banknotes feature a series of designs and motifs that represent the country's history, culture and biodiversity.The obverse of the banknote features the image of an important figure in Mexico's history, while the reverse features an image of a historical site or monument, a natural landscape or an emblematic animal of the country.Below, we will analyze the banknotes that are produced by \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico (Banxico)\uc2 , which has been responsible for their production since their creation in 1925.The $20 bill, issued for the first time in 1977, presents on its obverse side the consummation of Mexico's Independence, and on its reverse side you can see the mangrove ecosystem with the Mexican crocodile and the chocolate heron of the Ka'an reserve, in Quintana Roo.The $50 bill, issued for the first time in 1992, depicts the historical period of ancient Mexico on its obverse side, while its reverse shows the ecosystem of rivers and lakes, with the salamander and corn in Xochimilco, Mexico City.The $100 bill, issued for the first time in 1994, presents the image of the Mexican poetSor Juana In\u233\'00\'E9s de la Cruz and on its reverse side the ecosystem of temperate forests with pine, oak and oyamel trees, in the Monarch Butterfly Reserve, in Michoac\u225\'00\'E1n.The $200 bill, issued for the first time in 2002, presents on its obverse side the process of Mexico's Independence, with Miguel Hidalgo y Costilla and Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Morelos y Pav\u243\'00\'F3n, and on its reverse side the El Pinacate Biosphere Reserve and the Gran Desierto de Altar, in Sonora.The $500 bill, issued for the first time in 1994, shows \uc2 Benito Ju\u225\'00\'E1rez\uc2 , former president of Mexico, on its obverse and the El Vizca\u237\'00\'EDno Biosphere Reserve in Baja California Sur on its reverse.The $1000 bill, issued in 1983, shows Francisco I. Madero, Hermila Galinda and Carmen Serd\u225\'00\'E1n, revolutionary drivers, on its obverse, while its reverse shows the jaguar ecosystem in the ancient Mayan city of Calakmul, in Campeche.Mexico's banknotes also include a number of security features, such as watermarks, micro texts, holographic elements, serial numbers and special inks that change color when tilted or viewed under different lights. These security features help prevent counterfeiting and protect the integrity of the banknote.As for coins, it is the Mint of Mexico that mints and markets coins and medals, through processes that comply with quality standards for the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  and public and private, national and international institutions.Although there are coins of five, 10 and 20 centavos, in Mexico's economic framework they are not really used in the monetary flow. The 50 centavo coin, originally issued in 1992, shows the National Coat of Arms in relief in the center, while the reverse shows the number fifty together with the cent symbol "\u162\'00\'A2". The one peso coin shows on its reverse the value and a stylization of the Ring of Radiance of the Sun Stone.The two-peso coins show on their reverse side their face value, as well as a stylization of the Ring of Days of the Sun Stone. The five-peso coin shows on its reverse side the face value, accompanied by a stylization of the Ring of Serpents of the Sun Stone. The ten-peso coin shows in the central part the circle of the Sun Stone, which represents Tonatiuh, god of the sun and leader of the Mexica people, using the mask of fire.As part of the security features of the coins of this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we find the embossed engravings that can be felt with the fingers, the perimeter rings found on the edges of the coin and the magnetic ink, which can only be detected with special equipment.Commemorative coinsAccording to the \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2 , the Mexican peso has had several commemorative coins throughout time. Some of the most outstanding are the following:Five-peso coin commemorating the 75th anniversary of the oil expropriation, minted in 2013.100-peso coin commemorating the bicentennial of the beginning of the War of Independence, minted in 2010.20-peso coin commemorating the centennial of the promulgation of the Political Constitution of the United Mexican States, minted in 2017.10-peso coin commemorating the centennial of the birth of Octavio Paz, minted in 2014.Five-peso coin commemorating the 50th anniversary of the founding of the National Institute of Anthropology and History (INAH), minted in 1989.Two-peso coin commemorating the bicentennial of the birth of \uc2 Benito Ju\u225\'00\'E1rez\uc2 , minted in 2006.The Mexican peso has been a historically volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to various internal and external factors.Despite fluctuations in its value, the peso has managed to maintain relative stability in recent years thanks to the implementation of responsible monetary policies by \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico.However\uc2 , global economic uncertainty, trade tensions and the domestic political situation in Mexico continue to be factors that may affect the peso's strength in the short and medium term. MO\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020230228ej2s007j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
