\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Breakfast with Carstens: a central banker in the eye of the storm\b0\par\par\uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 What would you ask \uc2 Agust\u237\'00\'EDn Carstens\uc2 ? The former governor of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  is in the eye of the storm that central bankers live in. The eye of the storm is the region of the calmest weather conditions, around which there are fierce winds and heavy rains. \uc2 Agust\u237\'00\'EDn Carstens\uc2  lives in Switzerland. He is the head of the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 , the BIS, known as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the world's central banks. There, the global inflationary crisis is processed in a unique way. It is an unprecedented phenomenon, which is hitting hard in rich and poor countries alike. It is something that puts in tension the relationship between central bankers, the political class and societies around the world, "these are difficult times, and the autonomy of central banks was conceived for circumstances like these. Not for times when everything is fine and calm. It is in difficult times that autonomy can make a difference.\par \par \uc2  It is important for central banks to provide a vision that goes beyond the short term".\par \par \uc2 He is sitting at the head of the table. It's a Mexican breakfast and he orders a barbacoa taco for dessert. He has lived in Basel since late 2017 and you can tell he misses Mexican food. He was in CDMX to celebrate a regional BIS meeting. He has a bit more gray hair. He is sparing and cautious when talking about Mexico, but he is in front of a group of journalists who put pressure on him. He praises the role played by Alejandro D\u237\'00\'EDaz de Le\u243\'00\'F3n and Victoria Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez Ceja at the head of \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , "each one has done an excellent job. Each one has his or her own style".\par \par \uc2 Topics come and go in a conversation that lasts two hours and overlaps with Mexico's match against Poland. Martinoli's voice reaches us in waves and sometimes floods us, but we don't miss anything. Carstens' x-ray of the Superpeso is much more interesting than a scoreless game. \uc2 Agust\u237\'00\'EDn Carstens\uc2  has had a front row seat to Mexico's recent economic history and has the ability to put things in perspective. In 1994, there was also a very strong peso, but the situation was very different, he recalls: "the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  was committed to defending an exchange rate level and the government was willing to do almost anything to prop up the value of the peso, for example, issuing billions of dollars in Tesobonos... now, the strength of the peso is not related to government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not spend reserves either..."\par \par \uc2 The topics are chained together and minutes later we find ourselves talking about the rate differential between \uc2 Banxico\uc2  and the Fed, is it necessary to maintain the six points? His answer goes far beyond the short term, "it is likely that in the coming months we will first see the effects of the rate hikes on the slowdown of the economy rather than on the reduction of inflation. It will be very interesting to observe what will happen then... I am optimistic that we will soon see lower inflation, I think we could be at levels of 4 or 5%, but it will be complicated to go from there to 2% or less".\par \par \uc2 Agust\u237\'00\'EDn Guillermo Carstens\uc2  has just turned 64. He has received many awards, including Best Governor of a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by The Banker magazine in 2012. In the next few days he will receive the King of Spain Economics Award. Thirty years ago, another Mexican received this distinction, Miguel Mancera. He recalls that at that time he participated in the elaboration of the reception speech. The theme was the role of economic openness in controlling inflation. "The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  was a promoter of that opening, it brought more competition and market discipline in many branches of the economy". He anticipates that his speech will deal with the challenges facing central banks. One of those has to do with cryptocurrencies. "People expect central banks to play a bigger role in innovation-related issues. What is happening with Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies, with the bankruptcy of FTX makes it clear that in many cases the supposed monetary decentralization is false. Companies like FTX play a central role and are not subject to any rules.... At the BIS we have innovation labs and we are working with central banks to respond to a reality where many people want digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The challenge is technological and regulatory, it is very important to solve the balance between privacy and the obligations that there may be in tax or security issues." Last night he flew to Switzerland. That quiet place that is so close, in the very eye of the storms.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221123eibn005uc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
