\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Is Bitcoin the recipe for Mexicans to face their inflation and pay their debts?\b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Inflation rises and \uc2 Banxico\uc2  would take action, a mix that may plunge the population into debt, although many might be thinking about bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 Key facts:\par \par \uc2 57% of households in Mexico have some kind of debt.Not only is inflation rising in the Aztec country, bitcoin adoption is also on the rise.Inflation is unstoppable in Mexico. The National Consumer Price Index (INPC) presented an increase of 0.41% during the second fortnight of September, bringing the annual rate to 8.76%, just below the inflation reported in the second fortnight of August, of 8.77%. Given this scenario, bitcoin (BTC) appears in the recipe for Mexicans to contain the rise in prices, and also to pay their debts.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's economic outlook for the remainder of the year is not encouraging. On the one hand, the Anti-Inflation and Deficit Package (PACIC), implemented by the government of \uc2 Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  since May, has been ineffective, according to media reports.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2  (\uc2 OECD\uc2 ) considered that the inflationary environment in Mexico will continue to be complex, and therefore expects the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Banxico) \uc2 to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The same is expected by 20 analysts consulted by Reuters, who anticipate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 Banxico\uc2 ) will raise its key interest rate from 8.5% to 9.25% to contain inflation, following in the footsteps of the Federal Reserve (Fed).\par \par \uc2 More than 50% of Mexicans are in debtRising interest rates coupled with crippling inflation is sending more and more Mexicans into debt using credit cards to pay for everyday needs.\par \par \uc2 In fact, 57% of Mexican households, or nearly 21 million people claim to have some type of debt.\par \par \uc2 Among them, most have outstanding credit card commitments, while others have personal or payroll loans. This was revealed by public accountant Melisa Murialdo, who analyzed statistical data on the Mexican population.\par \par \uc2 The data analyzed by the expert shows that Mexicans are borrowing more and more money to pay for essential items such as food and gasoline, necessities that have increased in cost much faster than average earnings. And with interest rates poised to rise again, it could be even harder to get out of crushing debt.\par \par \uc2 However, Mexicans have a particular perception about their level of indebtedness, as only 8 out of every 100 people feel they are very indebted, while the majority perceive themselves to be debt-free and only a small percentage of the population has enough to save.\par \par \uc2 Is Bitcoin part of Mexicans' anti-inflationary recipe?In the face of Mexico's bleak economic outlook, bitcoin has begun to appear on the horizon, as the country has raised its level of cryptocurrency adoption.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Chainalysis\uc2 ' Global Cryptocurrency Adoption Index 2022, Mexico is in the Top 30, climbing from 44th to 28th place globally in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Chainalysis\uc2  measures the performance of countries based on their population's use of different bitcoin services and other cryptocurrencies. According to its report, Mexico obtained a score of 0.323; which now ranks it 28th among the 159 countries analyzed.\par \par \uc2 Analysts note that poorer countries are adopting bitcoin more. And although it does not include Mexico among them, it seems to be clear that the population sees the cryptoasset as a way out of the economic adversities resulting from government monetary policies.\par \par \uc2 However, what is the realistic position of liquidating levels of indebtedness with bitcoin? according to Ibrahim Martinez, Financial Analyst at Key to Markets.\par \par \uc2 Martinez believes that despite its adoption in Mexico, bitcoin has suffered a decline from its all-time high of more than 73%. Considering the market structure, bitcoin is still far from breaking price limits that potentially put it above USD 30,000, let alone recovering this loss of more than 73% of its market capitalization. So buying and holding cryptocurrencies in the short term may not seem sufficient to mitigate the debts of each household's equity.\par \par \uc2 However, cryptocurrencies offer a number of alternatives such as trading through CFDs with regulated and award-winning financial institutions such as Key to Markets. A CFD and \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  broker, which will allow you to invest in more than 180 assets or financial instruments, including cryptocurrencies with the possibility of leveraging with little capital and being able to benefit even when the crypto market falls through its website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.keytomarkets.com/latam/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.keytomarkets.com/latam/}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the analysis of data from the National Institute of Statistics and Geography reveals that 99.5% of households in Mexico have some kind of asset. And although, so far, they have focused on retirement savings accounts (53.9%) and pension funds, it is possible that more Mexicans are thinking ahead by relying more on bitcoin.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221117eibh00e80\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
