\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 ECONOMIC AND FINANCIAL TRENDS : The heavyweight\b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The exchange rate remains around 20.00 pesos per dollar, which makes the Mexican peso one of the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  worldwide so far this year, at a time when the euro is already below 1 dollar, the Japanese yen went from 115 to 150 yen per dollar and even the Chinese yuan went from 6.70 to 7.20 yuan per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The reasons why the Mexican peso has remained strong against the dollar is due to the free floating of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Unlike the economic crises of the last century when the President decided whether the peso should be devalued or not, as of April 1, 1994, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  is autonomous and the President of the Republic cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its decisions.\par \par \uc2 Since the December 1994 crisis, \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2  opted for a free float of the peso against the dollar, so the peso can wake up one day at 19.80 pesos and the next day, due to something that happens inside or outside Mexico, the peso will wake up at 20.20 pesos and then go to 20.50 pesos and return to 20.00 pesos the following week.\par \par \uc2 In the past, when we had a fixed exchange rate, if domestic and foreign investors bought large amounts of dollars, commercial banks would turn to the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 , which was forced to sell its reserves, and when they were about to run out, there was no choice but to devalue. Today that does not happen, \uc2 Banxico\uc2  does not sell its reserves and consequently they are already at a level of around 200 billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 Particularly in the last few months, the peso has remained stable, as a result of the inflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from exports, tourism, remittances and oil sales, as well as investments made by foreigners in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 However, if during 2023 Mexico does not grow at 3.0% as estimated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tax collection falls, there will be the possibility that the rating agencies will lower the sovereign rating and in doing so, a large amount of dollars invested in Mexico will leave the country, which will cause pressure on the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 There is no problem if the debt increases, as long as tax collection increases in the same proportion, which can only happen if the economy really grows significantly next year, which will probably not happen due to the bad moment the United States is going through, as well as the reduction in local consumption due to high inflation.\par \par \uc2 For 28 years the President of the Republic has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the peso-dollar parity, although his decisions can trigger a strong depreciation, if he makes happy accounts and also increases strongly the public debt, as will happen in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Carlos L\u243\'00\'F3pez Jones\par \par \uc2 Director of Consulting at Tendencias.com.mx\par \par \uc2 Twitter: @Carloslopezjone\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221020eiak0060x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
