\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Fintech law falls "short" of regulating cryptocurrencies; experts call for outlining tax and data protection issues\b0\par\par\uc2 1,305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Four years after the publication of the Law to Regulate Financial Technology Institutions, better known as fintech law, and on the eve of the construction of a digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico (Banxico)\uc2 , specialists and industry participants agreed that although this regulatory framework regulates virtual assets, it is not enough for the full and healthy development of the ecosystem that operates these instruments.\par \par \uc2 During the Bitcoin, Mexico and the Digital Economy Forum, organized by the association Legions, different specialists gave their point of view on how the issue of cryptocurrencies should be regulated given the potential they have to advance financial inclusion and therefore the economic development of the country.\par \par \uc2 "There is a Fintech Law in Mexico that is the legal framework, but it is not enough to be able to talk about all the issues regarding cryptocurrencies. What do we have to do? I invite the administrative authorities to have a uniform criterion regarding the use of cryptocurrencies, to reach this we have to make an interdisciplinary and equally that dialogue is opened," detailed Rebeca \u193\'00\'C1lvarez, founder of the Loxical firm.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, with the publication of the Fintech Law , the issue of virtual assets took on regulatory relevance. For the financial system, \uc2 Banxico\uc2  indicated that it would not allow institutions to offer these instruments to the general public and they can only use the technology for internal matters.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the Tax Administration Service (SAT) took on anti-money laundering supervision for those platforms outside the financial system and for the purposes of preventing money laundering and terrorist financing.\par \par \uc2 For Vanessa Solis, director of the firm Vconsulting, the current regulatory framework that applies to cryptocurrencies does not cover the tax treatment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of these instruments and although the Procuradur\u237\'00\'EDa de la Defensa del Contribuyente (Prodecon) has issued guidelines in this regard, the fact that the issue is not within the law causes uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 "There are countries that, without the need to compromise the financial system, have created measures or regulatory frameworks in tax matters. It is important to adapt the frameworks to the business models, but we must be very careful because any excess of the law can slow down growth," commented Solis.\par \par \uc2 Solis explained that although the Prodecon has suggested the payment of Income Tax in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of these instruments, the reality is that there are several aspects that must be considered for this to happen, because, for example, sometimes trading is done in other parts of the world or in centralized or decentralized platforms, which makes it impossible to talk about withholding this tax.\par \par \uc2 In her speech, Silvia Matus, vice president of External Practice of the Mexican Institute of Public Accountants (IMCP), detailed that, if the cryptocurrency trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  generates profits, it is necessary to pay ISR despite the fact that it is not contemplated as such in the Mexican tax framework.\par \par \uc2 "The authority does not care if we have income from trading or, for example, selling tables. If you have legal profits you have to pay," Matus explained.\par \par \uc2 Rebeca \u193\'00\'C1lvarez emphasized that, in the event of contemplating to adapt the regulatory framework for cryptocurrencies, it is necessary that the concepts addressed by the law be as broad as possible so that new business models such as non-fungible tokens or other types of tokens can be covered.\par \par \uc2 "We need concepts that are as broad as possible, that are flexible and that go along with business models.... It's not only putting the legal framework in place, it's also applying it. Today we have regulators in \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , the National Banking and Securities Commission, the National Commission for the Protection and Defense of Users of Financial Services and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who need to understand the business models and that is extremely important," Alvarez said.\par \par \uc2 Treatment of personal data During the event that took place in the Senate of the Republic, Rodolfo Guerrero, legal representative and partner of the firm Coffee Law, highlighted the urgency of regulating the issue of digital assets and their relationship with the protection of personal data, as well as cybersecurity issues and digital rights.\par \par \uc2 "A principle of homologation must be followed.... The fact is to recognize what we have in the law, as well as the necessary reform paths so that those points that are part of this digital transformation, are clear and generate this famous democratization of technology," Guerrero said.\par \par \uc2 The specialist highlighted that, in terms of privacy of information in digital assets, it is necessary to reform the Federal Law for the Protection of Personal Data, especially so that there is a path on what to do with the digital identity of a deceased person.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, Guerrero highlighted the need to approve a general law on cybersecurity, in addition to being clear about digital rights in the context of the traceability of blockchain technology and the treatment of personal data.\par \par \uc2 "What we have to take care of is privacy and recognizing as part of a digital right, the right to anonymity because if we do not take into account those two figures, we are going to generate a major debate on privacy coins," Guerrero noted.\par \par \uc2 Anti-money laundering regulations, the complement In different parts of the world, such as Iceland, India and China, cryptocurrencies are prohibited; however, in other countries such as Japan, Sweden and El Salvador, these instruments have freedom of use.\par \par \uc2 For the specialists who were at the forum, a concern of governments that restrict the use of cryptocurrencies is because of the risk of these instruments for money laundering and terrorist financing, which they are working to limit before the requirements of \uc2 the Financial Action Task Force\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The issues (of cryptocurrencies) that matter at the moment are the \uc2 FATF\uc2  rules and the issue is that of cybersecurity, so that the funds are properly protected," detailed Etienne Luquet Far\u237\'00\'EDas, leader of Regulatory Affairs for \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bitso.\par \par \uc2 In his \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez, director of Talent Land Blockchain, severely criticized the narrative that cryptocurrencies are a focus of risk for money laundering, since, most cases of money laundering happen in the traditional financial system.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico sadly has the largest money laundering cases in the history of mankind and it's all been done in regulated financial institutions.... If \uc2 FATF\uc2 , authorities, central banks, all politicians are really interested in combating money laundering, why haven't the licenses of these big money launderers been cancelled. On the other hand, in cryptocurrencies you have a system that is born regulated and transparent", said Rodriguez.\par \par \uc2 For the director of Talent Land Blockchain, this narrative about the use of cryptocurrencies for money laundering causes authorities to focus on excessive policies against companies or platforms that operate these instruments, which causes a brake on the progress they can give to financial inclusion.\par \par \uc2 "If we look at it like in El Salvador, today more people have a Chivo wallet to access bitcoin and remittances and for the first time they have access to international payments.... In six months, that country managed to implement a system that has surpassed all the banks combined," Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez explained.\par \par \uc2 For Ricardo Lechuga, partner of the firm Garrido Licona, in order to have an adequate regulatory framework for cryptocurrencies it is necessary to take into account the government's stance on monetary policy, financial security, structure for financial inclusion and even the environment.\par \par \uc2 "In Mexico we are in a process of financial inclusion, which so far has not been achieved (less than half of the population is banked), cash is still used as the main method of payment, that is a very complex issue to be able to enter into the regulation of these issues," Lechuga added.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220424ei4o008oo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
