\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Stocks Close Lower; Peso Firms Against Dollar -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2021\par 22:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1635 ET - The IPC stock index closes down 0.2% at 51045 points, well above its session lows, with \uc2 America Movil\uc2  shares off 0.9%, and \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares down 2.5%. The peso firms to 20.66 to the US dollar, which was broadly weaker following gains of recent days. The government placed MXN15B in a syndicated auction of 3-year peso-denominated bonds due March 2025. The yield was 7.18%, 8bp above the previous close of the existing September 2024 bonds, says Banorte. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1427 ET - \uc2 Mexico's Finance Ministry\uc2  welcomes \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  ' decision to affirm the country's BBB- sovereign rating and stable outlook, which it says will "help to preserve favorable access to international and domestic financial markets for the public and private sectors." \uc2 Fitch\uc2  cites "stable and robust external finances," and notes that Mexico's debt-to-GDP ratio has increased less than those of other triple-B rated countries. On the downside, the ratings firm says limited fiscal spending to address the pandemic could constrain future growth and employment, while government policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  affects investment prospects. \uc2 Fitch\uc2  forecasts economic growth for \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 5.9% this year and 2.8% in 2022. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1401 ET - \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  now expects the Fed to begin raising rates in July, or even earlier, rather than its previous forecast of December 2022, with another increase in November followed by three in 2023 and three more in 2024, eventually bringing the Fed funds rate to 2.1%. "Relative to our baseline expectation, we see greater risks of three rate increases next year than one," DB says. The move reflects higher-than-expected inflation and DB acknowledges the liftoff could be delayed if it weakens. Labor tightness is another factor. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1359 ET - A portfolio manager says he is betting on housing stocks as he sees supply issues easing next year. "I like those stocks," says Andrew Slimmon, the head of the applied equity advisors team at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2  . "You could have really good earnings growth for these companies because there is clearly a secular demand for those stocks, and their margins probably do quite well." Fresh data showed that housing starts, a measure of US home-building, declined 0.7% in October from September amid rising material costs and labor shortages that drove up home prices. (dave.sebastian@wsj.com; @depsebastian)\par \par \uc2 1357 ET - The US dollar weakens a touch against the euro and by 0.7% against a broadly stronger yen. The WSJ Dollar Index falls 0.2%. Minh Trang, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , tells WSJ today's moves are "a bit of profit taking given the recent run of the dollar.... Today you're seeing yields drop a bit and the dollar moving in unison." Trang says the dollar has had a great run, up nicely yesterday and this week overall. Retail sales numbers yesterday support the dollar's strength, he adds. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1346 ET - Investors are likely anxious about the uncertainty about the Fed's incoming policy decisions, but Christmas, buoyed by strong spending, is coming, says Andrew Slimmon, the head of the applied equity advisors team at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2  . "I think the market is near term overbought, and there's some pretty high bullish sentiment readings, and I wouldn't be surprised that the market takes a breather from now until ... the week after Thanksgiving," Slimmon tells WSJ. He also notes that corporate fundamentals gleaned from earnings reports have been better than expected. (dave.sebastian@wsj.com; @depsebastian)\par \par \uc2 1315 ET - The stock performances of retail companies that reported earnings this week have been mixed. \uc2 TJX\uc2  gains 7.9% after the retailer posted earnings that exceeded analysts' expectations, while Target falls 4.6% even though results topped Wall Street estimates. Nevertheless, any pullback in retail stocks are buying opportunities, says Andrew Slimmon, the head of the applied equity advisors team at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2  . "If a company beats estimates, but the stock drops, I would not run out and sell those stocks," Slimmon tells WSJ. "Stocks don't go down for long when companies are doing better than expected." (dave.sebastian@wsj.com; @depsebastian)\par \par \uc2 1154 ET - Preliminary data shows US airlines carried 58.4M passengers in September, above the previous year's figure but still below prepandemic September 2019, the \uc2 US Bureau of Transportation Statistics\uc2  says. In September of this year, airlines carried 53.6M domestic passengers and 4.8M international passengers, according to the data. Airlines carried 72.6M passengers in September 2019 and 25.1M passengers in Sept. of last year, the BTS said. Final September data will be released on Dec. 16. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)ING sees the euro falling to $1.10 by 4Q 2022. "ING Sees Euro Falling to $1.10 by 4Q 2020 -- Market Talk," at 1554 GMT, misstated the year in the headline.\par \par \uc2 1122 ET - The Canadian dollar falls as October's inflation data was largely disappointing for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite consumer prices rising at the highest rate since February 2003, Monex Europe says. Canada's consumer price index increased 4.7% year-on-year in October, which was in line with forecasts at time when U.S. CPI rose to levels not seen since the early Nineties, while other October inflation readings are outstripping economists' already high expectations, Monex \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Simon Harvey\uc2  says. "Secondly, the core-common measure of CPI, regarded as one of the better measures of underlying inflation, stayed stable at 1.8% despite many expecting it to show increased underlying inflation pressures." USD/CAD rises to a five-day high of 1.2599 after the data from 1.2552 beforehand. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1054 ET - ING expects the euro to fall to $1.10 by the fourth quarter of 2022 from $1.1302 currently as it sees the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raising interest rates before the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  . The Fed may raise rates by next summer while the \uc2 ECB\uc2  is likely to hold rates, ING analysts say. U.S. rate rise expectations are some 40-50 basis points below the Fed's projections, they say. For \uc2 ECB\uc2  rates, nearly 30 bps of tightening was expected for 2022 at one point, they say. "We view that pricing as extreme and unlikely, although it may take eurozone inflation dipping into next spring--the German VAT hike rolling out of calculations helps--before the market backs away from that kind of pricing." (renae.dyer@wsj.com) Correctiond & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 11:49 a.m. ET. An earlier version misstated the year in the headline as 2020.\par \par \uc2 1036 ET - Hackers linked to the Iranian government are launching cyberattacks against critical infrastructure including hospitals, local governments and transportation systems, according to a joint advisory from the US, UK and Australian governments. The state-sponsored group has broken into victims' computer systems using vulnerabilities in Microsoft Exchange email servers and tools made by the cybersecurity firm \uc2 Fortinet\uc2  . The governments' advisory, which warned of data theft and ransomware, urges companies to back up their data and update their computer systems. (david.uberti@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 17, 2021 16:35 ET (21:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020211117ehbh003b0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
