\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Banxico\uc2  Law Changes Put Country Note at Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Mexico's credit quality could be at risk with the adoption of changes to the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  Act, which will now be voted on in the San Lazarus plenary, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warned, which currently assigns Mexico a note of "Baa1" with a negative outlook.\par \par \uc2 In a comment issued last night, the qualifier explains that, "over the years, she has assessed that Mexico's institutional strength is low, compared to its peers, given weak rule of law and corruption control," but clarifies that this "is somehow offset by an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the relatively strong quality of financial regulation."\par \par \uc2 It warns that with Senate approval, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy is jeopardized, which would "undermine the cornerstone of Mexico's macroeconomic stability and a key strength of its sovereign credit profile."\par \par \uc2 In addition, changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  law could have a negative impact on investor confidence at a time when private investment takes two years of contraction.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the Political Coordination Board determined that the item will be added to the work agenda of the last day to be sessions in the lower house.\par \par \uc2 It was of no use that hours earlier, at a virtual meeting, some of the country's leading bankers explained to Members of the Finance Committee the risks that the \uc2 Bank of Mexico (Banxico) \uc2 would take to pass the initiative to reform the law of this central body that would generally force it to buy the surplus dollars in cash that enter the economy.\par \par \uc2 The main one of these risks, they detailed, is that resources of illicit origin could enter, with the implications that this would have, including international sanctions, but also with impacts on the local financial system and the economy.\par \par \uc2 The virtual meeting included the Association of Banks of Mexico (ABM) headed by Luis Ni\u241\'00\'F1o de Rivera, but also issued their views by the directors of the main banks operating in the country: \uc2 BBVA\uc2 , Santander, Banorte, Citibanamex, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , Scotiabank, Inbursa, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bankers stressed that the autonomy of the \uc2 Banxico\uc2  would be violated and would impose functions already performed today by commercial banks, such as the purchase of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and mentioned the strict controls with which they operate, by the hand of the authorities, to avoid the risk of money laundering, something that, they said, it would take Time for \uc2 Banxico\uc2  to build.\par \par \uc2 "The initiative in any case has to make sure that these international agreements that we have signed are fulfilled and that is why the Mexican banking system has been in the last 10 years, as a whole we have made great progress in complying with international best practices in money laundering," said Eduardo Osuna, Vice President of ABM and BBVA Mexico.\par \par \uc2 He stressed that the important thing was to ensure that the initiative complied with international treaties and that the autonomy of the \uc2 Banxico\uc2  was ensured, since, he said, it was a pillar of the financial system and the economy as a whole. "And by putting the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  at risk, we put the entire Mexican economy at risk."\par \par \uc2 Manuel Romo, director of Citibanamex, commented that if this initiative became law, not only the \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , but the entire financial system, would operate with a lower standard of control, make it risky and would be a setback.\par \par \uc2 "The initiative increasesrisk to the Mexican financial system, as it obliges the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an activity not only regulated, but strengthened by the conditions that, in practice, are handled between participants and that force the industry to have the strictest controls in the field of money laundering prevention that generates certainty not only in both parts of the chain , but also protects the international financial system from such crimes," he said.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Rojo, vice president of ABM, mentioned that the consequences of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  being part of an investigation into money laundering, by receiving surplus dollars in cash as suggested by the initiative, would create a systemic risk that could immediately expand to the entire financial system, limiting its capacity to act with other markets and thus the proper functioning of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Marcos Ram\u237\'00\'EDrez, director of Banorte, said that today we do not see a problem in the cash dollar market that makes it necessary to incorporate the most important risk factor into international financial flow relations.\par \par \uc2 Jorge Arce, director of HSBC Mexico, emphasized that the initiative as it is, and already approved by the Senate, puts the country at risk, and that it is economically unworkable by requiring unlimited purchase of dollars at the official exchange rate. "I'd make it a dollar magnet."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez-Ostos, vice president of the ABM and director of Barclays, stated that this initiative jeopardizes the autonomy of \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , which in turn would have a very negative impact on the perception not only of foreign banks, but of the market in general.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign banks are under very specific scrutiny of money laundering and counter-terrorism, and transferring that risk to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would put us in an atypical situation."\par \par \uc2 Unprecedented in the worldH\u233\'00\'E9ctor Grisi, executive chairman and CEO of Santander Mexico, stressed that there is no precedent in the world, that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the ultimate taker of some country's resources, which would pose a risk, because it is transferred to the State, responsibility for the purchase of cash and is also exposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that may be from the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  , which could lead to the freezing of international reserves.\par \par \uc2 eduardo.juarez@eleconomista.mx\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201215egcf0032e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
