\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Bankers explain to Members the risks that the \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , the financial system and the economy would take, if reform were passed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,010 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Some of the country's leading bankers explained to Members of the Finance Commissionthe risks that the \uc2 Bank of Mexico (Banxico) \uc2 would take to pass the initiative to reform the law of this central body and that, in general, it forces it to buy the surplus dollars in cash that enter the economy. The main one of these, they detailed, is that resources of illicit origin could enter, with the implications that this would have, even international sanctions, but also with impacts on the local financial system and the economy.\par \par \uc2 It was a virtual meeting in which the Association of Banks of Mexico (ABM) led by Luis Ni\u241\'00\'F1o de Rivera participated, but in which the directors of the main banks operating in the country also issued theirviews: \uc2 BBVA\uc2 , Santander, Banorte, Citibanamex, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , Scotiabank, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 For about three hours, bankers stressed that \uc2 Banxico\uc2 's autonomy would be violated, and that it would impose functions that commercial banks already perform today, such as buying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and mentioned the strict controls with which they operate, by the hand of the authorities, to avoid the risk of money laundering,something that, they said, it would take \uc2 Banxico\uc2  time to build.\par \par \uc2 "The initiative in any case has to ensure that these international agreements that we have signed are fulfilled and that is why over the last 10 years the Mexican banking system, as a whole, banks and regulators have made great strides in complying with international best practices in money laundering," said Eduardo Osuna, Vice President of ABM and BBVA Mexico.\par \par \uc2 He stressed that the important thing was to ensure that the initiative complied with international treaties and that the autonomy of the \uc2 Banxico\uc2  was ensured, since, he said, it was a pillar of the financial system and for the economy as a whole. "And by putting the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  at risk, we put the entire Mexican economy at risk."\par \par \uc2 Manuel Romo, director of Citibanamex, commented, for his part, that if this initiative became law, not only the \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , but the entire financial system, it would operate with a lower standard of control, make it risky and would be a setback.\par \par \uc2 "The initiative increases the risk to the Mexican financial system by forcing the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an activity not only regulated, but strengthened by the conditions that are, in practice, handled between participants and that force the industry to have the strictest controls on money laundering prevention that generates certainty not only in both parts of the chain , but also protects the international financial system from such crimes," he said.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Rojo, vice president of the ABM mentioned that the consequences of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  being part of an investigation into the issue of money laundering, by receiving surplus dollars in cash as suggested by the initiative, would create a systemic risk that could immediately expand to the entire financial system, limiting its capacity to act with other markets and thus the proper functioning of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Marcos Ramirez, directorBanorte, said that today from this bank there is no problem in the cash dollar market, which makes it necessary to incorporate the very important risk factor into international financial flow relations.\par \par \uc2 Jorge Arce, director of HSBC Mexico, emphasized that the initiative as it is, and already approved by the Senate, puts the country at risk, and that it is economically unworkable by requiring unlimited purchase of dollars at the official exchange rate. "It would make a magnet of dollars not only Mexican but global, to be washed here in Mexico."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ra\u250\'00\'FAl Mart\u237\'00\'EDnez-Ostos, vice president of the ABM and director of Barclays, stated that this initiative jeopardizes the autonomy of \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , which in turn would have a very negative impact on the perception not only of foreign banks, but of the market in general.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign banks are under very specific scrutiny of money laundering and counter-terrorism, and transferring that risk to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would put us in an atypical situation."\par \par \uc2 There is no precedent in the world\par \par \uc2 H\u233\'00\'E9ctor Grisi, executive chairman and CEO of Santander Mexico, stressed that there is no precedent in the world, that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the ultimate taker of some country's resources, which would pose a very significant risk, because it is transferred to the State, responsibility for the purchase of cash and is also exposed to having \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that may be from the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  , which could, in turn, lead to the freezing of international reserves.\par \par \uc2 He detailed that assembling all the systems to prevent money laundering that commercial banks have would take a long time to banxico. "Transferring this situation to the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  would be tremendously complicated, there is no precedent for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  having such risks."\par \par \uc2 Banxico\uc2 's autonomy is already in question\par \par \uc2 For Javier Foncerrada, CEO of Inbursa, the autonomy of the \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , regardless of whether or not the law is changed, is already in question, because a perception has been created that the autonomy of the organism is not so clear.\par \par \uc2 "That autonomy can be discussed and can be dimensioned (...) it seems to me that it is delicate that the autonomy of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  should be discussed; I think it's an unnecessary risk," he said.\par \par \uc2 Emilio Romano, director of Bank of America Mexico, said that although a solution is required, the initiative is not optimal, because, he said, the consequences could be very serious.\par \par \uc2 They propose alternatives\par \par \uc2 For representatives of the system's largest banks, the problem faced by some people in selling their dollars has a solution, as banks can buy up to certain amounts with all the necessary preventive measures, and on the issue that some institutions cannot repatriate them to their home countries, a bilateral meeting with the United States and other countries was suggested , involving authorities, to address the problem and seek agreements.\par \par \uc2 rrg\par \par \par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201215egce000av\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
