\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Banxico\uc2 , willing to help financial institutions meet law requirements for  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 804 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The Governor of the Bank of Mexico,Alejandro D\u237\'00\'EDaz de Le\u243\'00\'F3n,proposed three alternatives to the LXIV Legislature of the Union Congress to resolve the management of residual cash \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than the reform to Articles 20 and 34 of the Law of \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  that is currently under discussion in the Chamber of Deputies.\par \par \uc2 At an extraordinary meeting convened by the Finance and Public Credit Committee of the Chamber of Deputies,the central banker stated that measures can be implemented to help open more correspondent doors for all Mexican intermediaries, such as the creation of a kind of Council, where \uc2 Banxico\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would participate.\par \par \uc2 A second alternative would be for the Legislature to promote in law the use of technological tools developed by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 And the third would be the holding of financing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the same \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the multi-banking institutions that face difficulties in placing the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they capture in the country of origin.\par \par \uc2 The proposals are included in a document that was already submitted and delivered by \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2  to the chairman of the Finance Commission of the House of Senators, Alejandro Armenta Mier,on December 8, a day before they approved the reform of articles 20 and 34 of the Law of \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 , where they explain in detail each of them.\par \par \uc2 This same document with comments could already be in the hands of the Finance Committee of the Chamber of Deputies, taking into account the commitment of the central banker to support them in their deliberations in order to take longer to analyze the proposed changes in the minutes approved by the Senate. Time where they could listen to more experts and authorities in the field.\par \par \uc2 Alternative 1: The CouncilAs described in the document before the Senate Finance Commission, the copy of which is in the possession of El Economista,the first alternative is to implement measures that help open more correspondent doors for all Mexican intermediaries.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are those contracted by one bank to authorize another to make low-cost transfers outside the territory where the former operates.\par \par \uc2 "Given the complexity of the phenomenon, its solution requires the coordination of all relevant authorities (...) it would also be useful to consider the relationship to be had with the foreign authorities affecting the cross-border \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 If addressed, the initiative could be strengthened by creating a special coordinating authority to address this problem and could operate as the Financial System Stability Council,the Financial Inclusion Board and the Financial Education Committee.\par \par \uc2 Alternative 2: Technology PlatformThe Legal Team of the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  proposed as a second alternative that could be raised to the rank of law: the use of tools developed by the Central Institute to support institutions in carrying out their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with instruments considered to be of greater risk.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  can help mitigate the cause of the problem by creating a technology platform that centralizes \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction information infinancial institutions can know statistical data on the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  their clients carry out with other entities in the financial sector."\par \par \uc2 This "would aim to enable them to make a deeper and more complete due diligence process. Financial institutions would consult that platform to complete their customer knowledge process using the information they receive."\par \par \uc2 Using it "reduces the possibility of cash dollars that cannot be repatriated to the United States and thus the need for the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as proposed in the initiative."\par \par \uc2 Banxico\uc2 's legal team clarifies that if this proposal is advanced, "Mexican entities providing such services will be in a position to demonstrate to their counterparties that they serve as correspondent banks in the United States that their customer knowledge and due diligence processes are robust enough to maintain financial relations with it."\par \par \uc2 Alternative 3: FinancingThe third alternative proposed by \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2 's legal team is "to hold financing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with multiple banking institutions that face the conditions that make it impossible for them to place in the country of origin the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they capture, derived from the services they have been offering on a daily basis".\par \par \uc2 "This in order to avoid ingesting the acceptance of this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by such institutions thereby avoiding public impacts on access to foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cash transactions."\par \par \uc2 In turn, these transactions would mitigate the impact of the institutions' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions by the conditions described above and, on the other hand, would prevent \uc2 Banco de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2  from taking the risks acquired by such institutions that have resulted in the inability to place in their country of origin the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  acquired by them.\par \par \uc2 ymorales@eleconomista.com.mx\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201213egcd00593\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
