\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 12,500 mdd local bond market left in three months: \uc2 IMF\b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Mexico is characterized by being one of the emerging countries with the highest participation of foreigners in financial markets, which is a risk factor in the face of financial uncertainty and volatility is a report from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The Fund states that over the years, emerging and border markets have become more dependent on foreign capital flows. Specifically since the global financial crisis, 10 years ago, the participation of foreigners in emerging financial markets has grown significantly.\par \par \uc2 The stock market highlights, where non-resident participation has traditionally been smaller and that investors now own a significant share of stocks traded on stock exchanges in emerging economies.\par \par \uc2 Risks related to portfolio flows may be sharper in a context of high levels of global debt in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Two separate expert analyses of the Fund emphasize that proportions are "exposure factors in the event that financial conditions and investor preferences continue to be tightened".\par \par \uc2 In their "Covid-19 Response Policy Tracker," developed by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  for 191 countries and updated daily, they estimate that between February 20 and May 21, foreign investors settled Mexican government bonds for $12.5 billion or 1.2 points of Gross Domestic Product (GDP).\par \par \uc2 This proportion has increased since March, when the cumulative exits since the pandemic began in Mexico were equivalent to 0.4% of the product.\par \par \uc2 There they emphasized that in periods of high risk aversion, such as those experienced in March and mid-April by Covid-19, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have weakened as a result of sales by foreign investors who want to reduce their exposure and prefer to move to safer assets.\par \par \uc2 Within the second part of its Global Financial Stability Report (GFSR), \uc2 IMF\uc2  experts propose to limit risk from strengthening the emerging capital recipient, in this case Mexico or Hungary.\par \par \uc2 They propose to the authorities the development of efficient money markets, strengthen primary market practices to improve transparency and predictability of issuance, strengthen market liquidity, develop a strong market infrastructure as well as ensure compliance with the law and respect for contracts.\par \par \uc2 By ensuring certainty they fuel the confidence of investors who even in volatility can wait before selling or turmating securities, they said.\par \par \uc2 They emphasize that increased foreign participation in emerging debt markets exposes them to changes in global financial conditions through the behaviour and preferences of foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 They emphasized that in periods of risk aversion, when local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken and domestic assets are topped off, investors continue to reduce their exposure, triggering capital outflows that could disrupt bond markets.\par \par \uc2 The risk lies in the economies that were financed in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , generating what is called "original sin," they stressed.\par \par \uc2 Upward riskIn the Response Policy Tracker, Mexico chapter, they emphasized that the risk paid by 10-year sovereign titles rose from 131 points on February 20 to 434 basis points at the peak reached on April 28.\par \par \uc2 This"driven by \uc2 Pemex\uc2 's deterioration in risk" which reached 1,186 basis points from 377 starting points.This perception of uncertainty about Mexico and its state production company, led to a depreciation of the Mexican peso from May 18 to 21, which has eased from the peak reached of 25% at the end of last March, they detailed.\par \par \uc2 These capital outflows from \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  led to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a monetary-rate stock package implemented by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  in conjunction with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to try to order movements that are documented by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and which is one of the most modest of those collected in the world, equivalent to 0.7% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 [email protected]\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200525eg5p001xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
