\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican President Welcomes  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Rate Cut -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2020\par 15:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0911 ET - Mexican President \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  welcomes the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  's decision to lower interest rates to 7% from 7.25%, as it will make credit more accessible, at the same time tipping his hat to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's concern for keeping inflation in check. "Inflation control is extremely important," he says, because higher prices eat into wage increases. "We want the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make its decisions professionally, technically, without political \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hence our respect for the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  's autonomy." All five \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board members voted in favor of the 25bp rate cut, which was the fifth in a row. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 ET - Sterling will remain weighed down by concerns about U.K.-EU trade-deal talks in the coming weeks even if Britain's government delivers a fiscal stimulus boost, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  says. While the pound will be supported by expectations for an increase in government spending in the March 11 Budget, the market will be "keeping one eye firmly on politics" as forthcoming Brexit trade negotiations are "likely to be tough," Foley says. "Given the threat of a poor deal or even no deal for the U.K. at the end of the post-Brexit transition phase, we see GBP as remaining vulnerable," she adds. \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  expects GBP/USD to trade below 1.30 in the next one to three months. It is last down 0.2% at 1.3022. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 ET - \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  expects the euro's recent weakness to persist through the first quarter of 2020 before staging a recovery on improved eurozone data and a softening of the "overvalued" dollar. "We remain positive euro for the year as a whole, but short-term risks may remain on the downside," \uc2 BofA\uc2  analysts say, referring to market expectations that the coronavirus outbreak will hurt the eurozone economy in the first quarter. "We would expect a rebound in second-quarter data, but this means that we may get eurozone data that will be clean from the coronavirus only in the second half of the year." \uc2 BofA\uc2  expects EUR/USD to end 2020 at 1.15, up from 1.0848 now. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 ET - Eurozone government bond auctions next week will see Germany launching a March 2022-dated treasury note on Tuesday and Spain's launch of a January 2025-dated bond on Thursday. Slovakia kicks off sales Monday with EUR250 million on offer in June 2028 and March 2037 bonds. Germany's second auction will see EUR1.5 billion on offer in August 2048-dated Bunds on Wednesday. On Thursday, beside launching a new five-year bond, Spain will tap April 2030-dated debt with the offer volume to be announced on Monday. Also on Thursday, France will sell EUR7.25 billion to EUR8.75 billion in 2023-, 2025- and 2026-dated OATs and EUR1 billion to EUR1.5 billion in 2025, 2029 and 2036 inflation-linked bonds. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0845 ET - Brazil's economy is improving only gradually, with weak results from industry, services and retail sales in December leading to a contraction in December, according to Marcos Ross, senior economist at XP Investimentos in Sao Paulo. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's IBC-Br index, a monthly measure of economic growth, fell a seasonally adjusted 0.27% in December, and rose 1.28% from a year earlier. XP estimates Brazil's GDP expanded 0.5% in the fourth quarter and grew 1.5% from the final quarter of 2018. Given the outlook for inflation staying under control and a weak recovery, markets are likely to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its benchmark lending rate more, but the bank will probably leave the rate at the current record low of 4.25%, Mr. Ross said. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 ET - US consumers appeared to maintain a solid streak of spending in January, as retail sales rose a seasonally adjusted 0.3% from December and 4.4% from the year prior, according to a Commerce Department report. It was the fourth consecutive monthly increase, although sales in January were uneven. Food and drinking establishments, furniture stores and building material suppliers saw strong gains, while sales at electronic stores fell sharply. Clothing stores were also a weak spot in the report: Sales were down 3.1% in January compared to the prior month. That was the biggest monthly decline since March 2009, according to Commerce. (amara.omeokwe@wsj.com; @theamarareport)\par \par \uc2 0747 ET - The Hungarian forint should recover against the euro in coming weeks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday said it would take necessary steps to bring inflation back to its 3.0% medium-term target, ING says. Investors started reducing bets against the forint following remarks by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor \uc2 Marton Nagy\uc2 , ING analysts say. The bank will likely raise the Budapest Interbank Offered Rate to address high inflation, which "should keep HUF stable/modestly stronger in coming weeks." ING has closed its short EUR/HUF recommendation, opened Thursday, taking a 0.98% profit after the pair breached its 335 target. EUR/HUF falls 0.6% to 335.475. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0717 ET - The resignation of Sajid Javid as U.K.'s Chancellor of the Exchequer on Thursday opens the door to bumper fiscal spending, which poses a risk to \uc2 Citi\uc2 's gilt issuance forecast of GBP165 billion for fiscal year 2020-21. This is given the "uncertainty over the timing of capital spending," as \uc2 Citi\uc2 's forecast assumes it will be largely back-loaded into future fiscal years. But "the possibility of looser current spending--with tax cuts possibly back on the agenda--clearly adds to that," \uc2 Citi\uc2 's Jamie Searle says. The U.K.'s net financing requirement in FY2019-20 stands at GBP142.8 billion, after being revised upward twice. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0713 ET - The Polish zloty is likely to weaken against the euro in coming months as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refuses to address soaring inflation, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  , which expects EUR/PLN to rise to 4.40 from 4.2483 now. Official data on Friday showed Polish inflation rose to 4.4% year-on-year in January, its highest since 2011 and well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range of 2.5% plus or minus one percentage point. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will find it "increasingly difficult" to defend the notion interest rates should remain unchanged until 2022, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  's \uc2 Piotr Matys\uc2  says. Insisting that inflation is not an issue may "eventually undermine the credibility" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and this would have "negative implications" for the zloty. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 ET - Fiscal rules on current spending introduced by Sajid Javid before his resignation as U.K.'s Chancellor of the Exchequer may well be broken, prompting gilt investors to price higher supply risk, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  . The fiscal rules, "proving somewhat of an inconvenience, may well be broken only months after being adopted. If so, it's likely to be via some kind of fudge," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  's Jamie Searle. Javid's fiscal rules allow for an extra GBP20 billion pounds of infrastructure spending but require day-to-day spending and revenue to be in balance in the third year of any forecast. The 10-year gilt yield trades last at 0.626%, having reached almost a three-week high of 0.660% after Javid's resignation, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0623 ET - The Swiss franc will likely maintain its strength against the euro in the coming months due to its role as a safe haven asset, Rabobank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  says. "Even if coronavirus fears lessen, the recent worsening in eurozone economic data threatens to keep the CHF stronger for longer," she says "It is our expectation that eurozone growth will be sluggish this year implying that EUR/CHF is likely to continue to trade heavily." Rising worries about the global economic impact of the coronavirus outbreak are "also likely to benefit the franc." \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  has a three-month forecast of 1.06 for EUR/CHF, which is last up 0.1% at 1.0632. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 ET - Some pain from the novel coronavirus will be inevitable in Germany, \uc2 Gilles Moec\uc2  , chief economist for AXA Investment Managers, says. Even when it is still difficult to estimate the economic impact of the pathogen, there's growing evidence of its significant negative economic effect and Germany is highly dependent on Chinese demand for goods as it is its biggest export market, Moec says. "Beyond the impact of the epidemic, we have been for a while uncomfortable with the German economy's rising reliance on Chinese demand," Moec says. From a capital-expenditure perspective, China is by far the biggest economy in the world and given Beijing's strategy to reduce the capital intensity of its economy, German industry would be at risk even without the coronavirus, Moec says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 14, 2020 09:11 ET (14:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200214eg2e002eu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
