\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Llerenas and monetary policy\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The fundamental mistake is to imagine that central banks have the ability to determine, at their discretion, the level of interest rates. False.\par \par \uc2 In the case of monetary policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanisms, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has control over a very short-term rate only.\par \par \uc2 The British economist Keynes wrote in his masterful treaty The Economic Consequences of Peace in \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Central Banking an expert in a million, but we find it every day. This is the case of the guest columnist Vidal Llerenas, who, coming out of his journalistic comfort zone (the issues of urban development) reopened a flank of vulnerability to show in writing his improvisation in monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Great misinformation in that field and nothing else shows in writing that "the demand on the [\b \highlight22\uc2 central] bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to be to lower interest rates at a faster rate." The fundamental mistake in this stark statement is to imagine that a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the capacity to determine discretionary interest rates in the economy. False. In the case of monetary policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanisms, the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  has control over a very short-term rate only. Should such a rate be determined at a level inconsistent with the general conditions prevailing in the economy, the error would be perversely manifested in a term interest rate structure with a very steep slope. That is, with the rates with which investment projects are financed at very high levels, inhibiting economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Another big mistake in the article "El \uc2 Banxico\uc2  and its comfort policies" is not making the distinction between inflation and its expectations or forecasts. They're not the same. If even if inflation is in its target range, the corresponding expectations are not sufficiently anchored with their long-term goal, it is not possible to sing victory prematurely by vocingly announcing that "inflation is controlled." The fact is that those expectations are not yet sufficiently anchored.\par \par \uc2 I do not believe that any name-worthy central banker could propose that interest rates be kept at an artificially high level to be kept in a "comfort" zone. Nor do I think Werner of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  would commune with such a dislate.\par \par \uc2 Werner surely knows that in order to keep interest rates down in a sustainable way, they need to be relatively high at the juncture to anchor inflation expectations. But there was no way it would support the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling in haste for that purpose.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200206eg260029n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
