\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 US pushes fund for 10,000 MDD for "new government" of Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2019\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY.- The United States leads a group of 20 countries working on a $ 10 billion aid package in commercial financing for Venezuela. The objective, said \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2 , secretary of the US Treasury, is that the new government of \uc2 Juan Guaid\u243\'00\'F3\uc2  can get out of the economic and humanitarian crisis that the country is experiencing. Guaid\u243\'00\'F3 has been recognized by 50 countries while President Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro has the support of Russia and China. That has plunged the country into a chaos that has driven 3.7 million people to leave Venezuela in recent years due to the shortage of food and medicine and which has left their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worthless in addition to their economic output is expected to be contract 25% this year. The financing is to reverse that crisis, Mnuchin said in Washington, during a meeting of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "We are going to work to try to put together a consortium of around 10 billion dollars of commercial financing that would be available for the new government to boost trade." Among the countries participating in the initiative are Germany, Argentina, Brazil, Canada, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Ecuador, Spain, France, Guatemala, Guyana, Portugal, Japan, Panama, Peru, and the United Kingdom, according to the statement issued for the Treasury. Mexico and Italy also participate although they have not recognized Guaid\u243\'00\'F3. In this regard, Arturo Herrera, undersecretary of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Public Credit (SHCP) said at a press conference that Mexico is not putting "proposals on the table" in relation to Venezuela. "Mexico's position is very clear and it is a position of no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  favors" a negotiated solution "to the political crisis, he insisted. IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  reiterated the agency's "extreme concern" for Venezuela, but said that they can only be "guided by membership." "It's not about us deciding, it has to be a large majority of the members that recognize, diplomatically, the authorities they consider legitimate, and as soon as that happens, we will act," he told reporters. (With information from agencies)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020190414ef4e000mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
