\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Rodolfo Navarrete Vargas / Will increase the rate\b0\par\par\uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2018\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The exchange rate continues to be a cause for concern in Mexico. During the past week, it once again observed worrying behavior not only because it was the second worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  among the emerging countries, but also because it has been observing this performance for just under two months. This time could be enough to start influencing inflation, especially since it has become more sensitive to this kind of events, due to the fact that the economy is in the high phase of the cycle. Last week the Mexican peso depreciated 2.76%, just below the South African \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that did it at 3.01 percent. Since April 17, the date on which the weakening of practically all emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  began, those that depreciated the most are the Argentine peso (20.65%), the Mexican peso (12.23%), the Brazilian real (9.18%) and the Turkish lira (8.72%). This phenomenon is due to the combination of two types of factors.\par \par \uc2  One, the attraction of capital to the United States, mainly related to the Fed's monetary policy; and, the second, the expulsion of capital from emerging countries, which depend basically on the state of the economic foundations of each country. The US monetary policy runs on two lanes. One, the rise in the reference interest rate; and, the other, the decrease in the Fed's balance, which implies the reduction of the liquidity of the system, which acquires disturbing characteristics in circumstances in which the need for money is increasing as a result of the increase in the US fiscal deficit. This attracts capital from all over the world, forcing global investors to sell their risky asset positions, especially in emerging countries. On the other hand, the depreciation observed by these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not accidental. It responds to the relatively vulnerable state of the macroeconomic foundations of each of these countries. Most of them have problems with their fiscal balances and with the current account of their balance of payments, as well as with inflation. However, the problem in Mexico is different. The main internal headache is the commercial relationship with the United States. Once the attempt to reach an agreement on \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  that the \uc2 American Congress\uc2  could approve this year has failed, a wide range of negotiating possibilities has opened up, which in the end only contribute to raising uncertainty. The reimposition of tariffs by the United States to imports of steel and aluminum from Mexico, Canada and the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , as well as the retaliatory measures adopted by these countries, increase the probability of a commercial war in the world and that Trade negotiations between Mexico and the United States will be complicated. This is the factor that internally explains the depreciation of the peso, to which probably has been added the concern regarding the elections of July 1. Finally, it should be noted that the weakening of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is observing some worrying characteristics. Apart from its high magnitude (12.2% since mid-April), and being the second \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the worst performance in emerging countries, the strong increase in peso volatility has been added to it. This is relevant because apparently the pattern of \uc2 Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 M\u233\'00\'E9xico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the recent past has had to do with the correlation between these two variables. That is to say, that whenever there was a strong depreciation of the peso, accompanied by a significant increase in the volatility of the exchange rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the monetary policy interest rate. This concern is even more important if we take into account the warning from the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  in its May 12 press release that, if necessary, it will act in a "timely and firm" manner to achieve the convergence of inflationto its goal of 3.0 percent. As a result, it would not be strange to see an increase in the benchmark interest rate before the monetary policy meeting scheduled for June 21. Director of Analysis and Investment Strategies Vector Casa de Bolsa.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020180611ee6b002ky\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
