\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Argentina surprises with hike; Mexico, Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  gain\b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2018\par 01:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Recasts with Argentina rate hike)\par     April 27 (Reuters) - Argentina's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surprised\par markets on Friday by hiking its key interest rate and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with dollar sales to support its peso, while\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's and Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gained ground after steep,\par recent losses.\par     Argentina's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unexpectedly raised its key rate by\par 3 percentage points to 30.25 percent after the Argentine peso\par slumped to an all-time low.\par     The bank said it sold $1.38 billion on the spot market on\par Friday. The moves helped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rocket back\par from its low to close little changed on the day.\par     Analysts said the market had been doubting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would hike interest rates while the government pushes\par policies aimed at spurring economic growth ahead of President\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mauricio Macri\f28 \uc2 \f2 's expected 2019 re-election bid. \par     Mexico's peso and Brazil's real firmed after mixed U.S.\par economic data drove investors to pare back bets on a faster pace\par of U.S. interest rate hikes.\par     The U.S. economy slowed in the first quarter as growth in\par consumer spending braked sharply, though labor costs increased\par more than expected in the period.\par     "Consumer spending was weak, despite a strong overall\par reading. We'll need more clues to justify the recent growth of\par rate hike bets," said Mauriciano Cavalcante, the head of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at Ourominas.\par     Emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have slumped in recent weeks due\par to expectations of accelerating inflation and a widening fiscal\par deficit that could drive the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  to accelerate\par policy tightening. That would likely dampen demand for\par high-yielding assets.\par     The Mexican peso firmed 0.8 percent, while the\par Brazilian real gained 0.4 percent.\par     Brazil's and Mexico's benchmark stock indexes were little\par changed, but shares of some companies made big moves following a\par  batch of corporate updates.\par     Shares in Mexican broadcaster \f28 \uc2 \f2 Grupo Televisa\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par rose more than 4 percent after it reported stronger sales and\par said it is considering spinning off assets, particularly its\par cable division.\par     Rival cable television provider \f28 \uc2 \f2 Megacable\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par jumped over 7 percent, its biggest one-day percentage gain in\par five years, after its report beat expectations.  \par     Mexico's Jose Cuervo, the world's largest\par tequila producer, fell more than 6 percent after publishing\par disappointing U.S. sales numbers. It was the stock's biggest\par one-day loss since it listed early last year.\par     Shares of Brazil's GPA SA jumped 3.95 percent\par after the food retailer beat first-quarter profit estimates on\par Thursday, fueled by a strong performance by its wholesale unit.\par \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 2100 GMT:\par    \par  Stock indexes                              daily %    YTD %\par                        