\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Mexico readying economic response if U.S. exits \uc2 NAFTA\b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2017\par 00:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Recasts introduction, adds comments from \uc2 IMF\uc2 , background)\par \par \uc2 By Ana Isabel Martinez\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Mexico's government is preparing a macroeconomic response in case U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  makes good on threats to quit the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  (\uc2 NAFTA\uc2 ), an event which could wreak havoc on the Mexican economy and hurt the peso.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's Foreign Minister \uc2 Luis Videgaray\uc2  said on Monday the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were preparing a plan to address the possibility of a future without \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 , but gave few details.\par \par \uc2 The government has said it is examining how it could adjust Mexican legislation to give investors certainty about their investments if the almost 24-year-old \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  collapses.\par \par \uc2 Underpinning some $1.3 trillion in annual trade between the United States, Canada and Mexico, \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  has been a central pillar of recent Mexican economic development. Nearly 80 percent of Mexican exports are shipped to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Trade negotiators from the United States, Mexico and Canada meet in Mexico City this week to continue talks on overhauling the accord, and Videgaray reiterated the government's position that the expectation was that talks would ultimately succeed.\par \par \uc2 Mexico would continue to work on diversifying trade, protect foreign investment, review possible changes to tariff barriers, and prepare a macro-economic response from the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Videgaray added.\par \par \uc2 "These are the four lines a plan B must include," he told Mexican radio. "We have to be prepared for all the scenarios and one of the scenarios is that the United States leaves the treaty, and as we have said, that is not the end of the world, the Mexican economy is much bigger than \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Separately, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said in a report on Monday that ending \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  would bring back \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  "most-favored nation" tariffs, which would disrupt Mexican-U.S. trade, and could crimp economic growth, dampen capital inflows and raise risk premia.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  suggested that among various policy responses at \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's disposal, "temporary \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and liquidity provision could help smooth extreme volatility."\par \par \uc2 Concerns that Trump could follow through on his threats to dump \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  have battered the Mexican peso in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, Mexico should continue to implement its structural reforms and boost efforts to diversify trading relationships, which would increase competitiveness and help economic growth over the medium-term, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  sees Mexico's economy growing 1.9 percent next year after projected expansion of 2.1 percent in 2017. (Additional reporting by Anthony Esposito; Editing by Paul Simao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-11-14T00:02:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020171113edbd00vez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
