\par\par \b \uc2 Trump Administration Considering Adding  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Rules to Nafta -- 3rd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By William Mauldin \par \uc2 1,032 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2017\par 21:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Trump administration has told senators it is considering adding rules barring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation to the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  , according to congressional aides, a departure from past U.S. policy that could set a precedent for other trade deals.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  said in a Senate meeting Wednesday that he is looking at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules as a part of planned renegotiation of Nafta, the 23-year-old trade agreement that connects the U.S. economy with Canada's and Mexico's, the aides said.\par \par \uc2 An administration official confirmed the U.S. is looking at some sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  disciplines but didn't say whether the rules would be binding and enforceable through Nafta's dispute-settlement procedures.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lighthizer is briefing key House and Senate lawmakers this week on the North American agreement, and the Trump administration is expected in the coming days to send Congress a letter announcing plans for formal Nafta negotiations. The talks, which could start in three months, would likely include Nafta updates to address technology and the internet, as well as stronger rules on what products qualify for duty-free trade in order to favor North American supply chains over rivals in Asia\par \par \uc2 Canada and Mexico haven't been the focus of U.S. criticism over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but adding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules to Nafta could set a precedent for including them in future negotiations with Asian trading partners.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules weren't in a previous draft of the administration's \uc2 Nafta\uc2  negotiating blueprint plan reviewed by The Wall Street Journal in March.\par \par \uc2 "It is highly controversial, but I think it does make sense," said Fred Bergsten, founding director of the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2  and co-author of a forthcoming book this year on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 "The link between exchange-rate movements is very clear and very substantial. It's a lot more important than many of the other things that have been linked to trade policy," he said.\par \par \uc2 While U.S. manufacturers have complained about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, groups representing sprawling multinational businesses say they are more concerned about tariffs and commercial rules of the road than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, which fluctuate up and down over time.\par \par \uc2 In the past two years, critics of U.S. trade policy have generated opposition to Washington's trade agreements by saying China and other countries manipulate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in ways that have a bigger effect on their export competitiveness than tariff levels set by trade deals.\par \par \uc2 Former President Barack Obama's administration didn't include enforceable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules in the Trans-Pacific Partnership, the 12-nation deal that Congress never ratified and President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  exited from in January. At the time, U.S. officials said that trading partners were opposed to the rules, and some worried such guidelines, even if carefully tailored, could tie the hands of U.S. monetary policy in the future.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the TPP countries agreed to nonbinding guidelines and transparency procedures outside the agreement.\par \par \uc2 The issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules wasn't unexpected as a part of the new administration's trade policy. Mr. Trump has frequently blamed China for manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although his Treasury Department declined to declare Beijing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator this year. Economists say China has actually been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the value of the yuan of late, rather than pushing it weaker to gain a trade advantage.\par \par \uc2 Trade legislation passed by Congress in 2015 directs the administration to look at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues in trade pacts. "Unless they address \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they're not going to get trade agreements through the Congress," Mr. Bergsten said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lighthizer is set to attend a gathering of Asian leaders in coming days in Vietnam. The Trump administration is seen as unlikely to revive the TPP but could pursue bilateral talks with individual countries in the bloc.\par \par \uc2 In Canada, a spokesman for Foreign Minister \uc2 Chrystia Freeland\uc2  said Canada is ready to negotiate new terms for Nafta once formal talks begin.\par \par \uc2 Neither the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  nor the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  targets a particular level for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considered only in "exceptional circumstances," according to material on the \uc2 Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's website. The last time the \uc2 Bank of Canada\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was in September 1998.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian dollar tends to move in line with the price of crude oil, given the importance of the commodity sector on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Mexico also allows the peso to float freely. The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has declined sharply when Mr. Trump has threatened to pull the U.S. out of Nafta.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation is often in the eyes of the beholder and neither \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  nor Canada has conducted extensive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Eswar Prasad, a Cornell University professor and former official at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  . Mr. Prasad said adding the provision could help with leverage in negotiations but "would mainly help in some political chest-thumping about getting tough with U.S. trading partners."\par \par \uc2 Led by the Detroit auto industry, U.S. manufacturers have complained that trading partners have pursued economic policies that weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make their exports unfairly competitive when shipped to the U.S., compared with American goods.\par \par \uc2 U.S. auto makers blamed Japan for taking steps that weakened the yen, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules most frequently discussed by trade officials wouldn't prevent countries from so-called quantitative easing or similar domestically focused steps to boost demand.\par \par \uc2 If incorporated into Nafta, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules would become a precedent that could be used in future trade talks with Japan, Malaysia, South Korea or other Asian countries that have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. Japan and Malaysia were part of the TPP talks, and South Korea has a free-trade agreement with the U.S. that the Trump administration has signaled it wants to renegotiate.\par \par \uc2 The rules could also be used in economic negotiations with China.\par \par \uc2 Paul Vieira in Ottawa contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 Write to William Mauldin at william.mauldin@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 17, 2017 15:43 ET (19:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170517ed5h002wz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
