\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican central banker says Trump's tweets modified peso strategy\b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2017\par 03:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, April 5 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said on Wednesday the bank altered course on how to protect the peso after a couple of tweets by U.S. President Donald Trump in early January pummeled the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to near historic lows and wiped out the effect of a $2 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Banco de \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early January had sold dollars to fight off the peso's nosedive to record lows amid fears the protectionist policy pronouncements of then President-elect Trump could further hammer Latin America's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 "I'll say it like this, in simple terms: with two tweets from you know who, the effect (of that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) vanished," Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Agustin Carstens said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "It was precisely then we thought that instead of using hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like dollars, it would be better to move to a scheme in which there was the possibility of offering hedges" without having to eat into Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Carstens added.\par \par \uc2 Carstens did not specify what tweets he was referring to.\par \par \uc2 But Trump in early January targeted \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors Co\uc2  with the threat of a "big border tax" over cars built in Mexico meant for U.S. consumers.\par \par \uc2 Since then, \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through dollar sales by implementing an exchange hedging program for up to $20 billion.\par \par \uc2 The bank earlier on Wednesday sold its entire offer of $200 million in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges in an auction in which demand far outstripped supply, reflecting appetite for the program aimed at supporting the country's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Carstens said that the peso , which fell to a low of nearly 22 per dollar following the U.S. elections in November but has since recouped most of those losses, is still undervalued and has room to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 Future interest rate decisions by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  should not cause further depreciation in the peso, Carstens added.\par \par \uc2 Regarding monetary policy, Carstens also said that depending on the evolution of inflation expectations, the \uc2 Banco de Mexico\uc2  may be able to stop following Fed decisions in tandem. (Reporting by Anthony Esposito and Sharay Angulo, editing by G Crosse) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-4-6T02:10:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170406ed46001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
