\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  Launches Dollar-Hedging Program--Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup \par \uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2017\par 20:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY -- The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  on Monday launched a dollar-hedging program, placing all $1 billion in forward contracts as it opens a new front in efforts to support the Mexican peso without draining foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $1 billion in nondeliverable forwards at six different maturities ranging from 30 days to 360 days. Overall demand was $2.08 billion, with average exchange rates ranging from 19.5793 pesos per dollar for the one-month contracts to 20.4904 pesos per dollar for the one-year contracts.\par \par \uc2 Unlike traditional forwards, in which participants agree to buy dollars at a set price on a future date, the nondeliverable forwards are settled in pesos. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will pay the difference between the contract rate and the exchange rate at maturity if the peso is weaker against the dollar, or receive the difference if the peso is stronger.\par \par \uc2 The discount compared with traditional forwards was "relatively tight, so there seems to be some dollar demand there," said Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Jaime, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates strategist at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in New York. "The good news is that this instrument seems to be attractive enough for people to use it."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hedging program is for up to $20 billion, although subsequent auctions have yet to be scheduled.\par \par \uc2 The use of derivatives gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ammunition to support the peso without selling dollars from its foreign reserves, as it has done in recent years. The bank sold around $23 billion from reserves in 2015 and early 2016, then suspended dollar auctions in favor of occasional direct \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to discourage speculation against the peso. \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves are currently around $175.1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Last month's announcement of the hedging program helped push the peso to its strongest level against the dollar in months.\par \par \uc2 "First it's a signaling mechanism, like telling the market 'hey guys, we have a way bigger number of dollars if we want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' without draining international reserves, and the second is the direct impact of actually offering $20 billion," Mr. Jaime said.\par \par \uc2 Mexican corporations have used \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging extensively since a number of companies suffered big losses in the 2008-2009 crisis. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's program will increase the supply as it offers forward dollar sales for the first time, said Jaime Cortina, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, there are many people with exposure to exchange-rate fluctuations. Importers, companies that have debt denominated in dollars that often seek to cover their risk, that would also benefit," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anthony Harrup at anthony.harrup@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 06, 2017 14:50 ET (19:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170306ed36002u8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
