\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican peso may slump to record low on Trump trade moves -traders\b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2017\par 23:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Jan 19 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso could sink to a record low after Donald Trump assumes the U.S. presidency on Friday if he unveils any aggressive actions that could hammer the exports of Latin America's No. 2 economy, traders and analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso has sunk more than 16 percent since Trump won the election. Trump threatened to rip up or renegotiate the \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  free trade deal with Mexico and Canada and impose tariffs on Mexican exports.\par \par \uc2 The peso hit a record low in January and traded close to that low just above 22 per dollar on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Traders and economists said the peso could shoot toward 23 per dollar or even 24 per dollar if Trump takes a harsh tone on trade in his inaugural address or announces any concrete measures, such as imposing tariffs on goods from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of anxiety in the market," said Marco Oviedo, an economist at Barclays in Mexico City, who said the peso could hit 23 per dollar if Trump would take any bold action.\par \par \uc2 "We don't know what is the value of the peso because we don't know what is the final policy. It has been really, really exhausting to imagine what he could do."\par \par \uc2 Trump is expected to sign an executive order in his first few days to direct the building of a wall on the southern border with Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso dropped 2 percent on Wednesday after Trump's choice for commerce secretary, Wilbur Ross, said renegotiating the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  would likely be the incoming administration's first priority. Mexico sends nearly 80 percent of its exports to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts said the peso would likely trade around current levels if Trump only confirms that idea of renegotiating \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The peso could rally on anything that suggests a firmer outlook for U.S. growth with no significant trade restrictions," said Goldman Sachs economist Alberto Ramos.\par \par \uc2 Trump's nominee to run the Treasury Department, \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2 , on Thursday said Washington can renegotiate \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  in a way that benefits both the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexican authorities have said they are prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market with dollar sales if peso movements becomes disordered. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $2 billion in early January to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the peso was moving to adjust to concrete plans by Trump that would affect Mexico in a fundamental way.\par \par \uc2 "The preference of the authorities is well known and it is to not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much in the exchange market," said Credit Suisse economist Alonso Cervera. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle, Roberto Aguilar, Noe Torres and Paulina Osorio; Editing by Phil Berlowitz) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-1-19T23:03:24.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170119ed1j0187z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
