\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Mexico \highlight\uc2  Sold $2B in Exchange  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2017\par 17:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 11:26 ET - The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $2B last week in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in almost a year, the bank's foreign reserves report shows. Reserves fell by $1.8B during the week to $174.7B. The peso hit a low against the US dollar after \uc2 Ford\uc2  (F) canceled a major assembly plant project, fueling fears of further limits on investment under the Trump administration in the US. The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on several days, although the peso has remained under pressure, recently trading in Mexico City at MXN21.5680 to the dollar vs MXN21.3695 Monday. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:25 ET - \uc2 Audi\uc2 's top US executive nods to competing auto makers' race to be first with self-driving-car technology to put a stamp on vehicles in coming years. \uc2 Audi\uc2  partnering with \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  (NVDA) to roll out consumer-ready autonomous vehicle in 2020. Tesla, too, has turned to \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  for computing power in its next-generation Autopilot, featuring hardware that can one day be upgraded to make its electric cars fully self driving, the company says. Scott Keogh, \uc2 Audi\uc2 's top US executive, contends central processing unit in coming \uc2 Audi\uc2  vehicles will set their self-driving technology apart. "We definitely want to do a bunch of things first. The reason we want to do them first is you get pricing power if you do them first and you get recognition for doing them first," he says at North American International Auto Show in Detroit. (mike.spector@wsj.com; @MikeSpectorWSJ)\par \par \uc2 11:23 ET - The number of ETF listings in the US hit an all-time high of just under 2,000 in 2016, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said Monday. The products have become increasingly popular in recent years because they trade daily on exchanges like stocks and have some tax benefits. Of the 15 most heavily traded securities last year by volume, only one was a stock--\uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (BAC)--while the rest were ETFs. And overall, ETFs accounted for about 30% of all US trading by value, the most since 2011, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said. The firm added that active funds have posted net outflows each year for the last decade while more than 40% of mutual-fund and ETF assets are now in passive funds. (sarah.krouse@wsj.com; @bysarahkrouse)\par \par \uc2 11:20 ET - The oil industry downturn that began in 2014 may or may not be over, but a tally of its layoffs keeps growing, reaching 440K globally through 2016, according to Houston-based \uc2 Graves & Co\uc2 . Tracking oil industry labor trends becomes more difficult as some sub-sectors recover, especially because hiring at public companies generally doesn't generate a public announcement like layoffs do, John Graves tells WSJ. But "while the business climate is improving -- particularly in the Permian Basin -- the outlook for the oilfield services companies working internationally and offshore is still grim." He notes \uc2 Rowan Companies\uc2  (RDC) laid off 80 workers in November as a drillship came off contract. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:17 ET - High-flying metals such as platinum, palladium, zinc and steel may be hindered in 2017 by a slowing auto market in China, following a reduction in a tax cut that helped push vehicle sales higher, analysts said. Platinum and palladium, which are used in catalytic converters, are up more than 8% and 11% respectively year to date. However, lower car sales in China in the coming months will likely hit prices said Carsten Menke at \uc2 Julius Baer\uc2 . Meanwhile, galvanized steel, which in turn makes up a lot of demand for zinc, would also be hurt if sales come down. "The concern amongst investors is that this [tax cut] has just pulled forward demand from 2017 and 2018," Citi analysts wrote. China makes up 26% of the global auto market and 60% of growth since 2008, according to Citi. (stephanie.yang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:11 ET - Zimmer (ZBH) reports better-than-expected 4Q sales, easing fears over management credibility after supply-chain problems caused surprising shortfall in 3Q, Citi says. Even though the beat helps "heal some of those wounds," Citi says the medical-device maker remains vulnerable to a broader "slowdown" in the US market for orthopedic devices. Citi recommends that ZBH holders sell and "become more aggressive profit takers" to take advantage of the market's new "optimism" over ZBH's 4Q beat. ZBH up 5.7% to $113.12, helping erase some of the stock's decline since the Q3 miss; ZBH off 14% over past three months. (joseph.walker@wsj.com; @joewalkerwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:06 ET - \uc2 UBS\uc2  calls Allscripts Healthcare's (MDRX) positive pre-announcement "particularly impressive" given the negative sentiment across health IT post-election on fears of an Obamacare roll-back. MDRX last night gave better-than-expected 2017 revenue guidance and topped analyst estimates for 4Q bookings. Citi says it's a "favorable read-through to peers," including \uc2 Cerner\uc2  (\uc2 CERN\uc2 ). It also says "solid top line 2017 guidance," seems to support its recent findings that large customers like hospitals don't seem to be drastically altering health IT demands. MDRX is expected to give more details about its guidance when it presents this afternoon at the JPMorgan Healthcare Conference in San Francisco. MDRX up 11% to $11.98 and \uc2 CERN\uc2  gains 2.4% to $49.14. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:05 ET - \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut its benchmark Selic rate by 0.5 percentage points, forecasts economist Jason Vieira, from asset-management firm Infinity in Sao Paulo. He said a deeper, 0.75 p.p. cut, as some other economists forecast, won't happen until there is more certainty government-proposed pension reforms will be approved in Congress. "There needs to be a more consistent background," for bigger cuts, he said. Pension reform is seen as key to re-balancing the public budget and taming a ballooning debt load. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 10:57 ET - Electric cars only make up a sliver of US auto sales--pickup trucks and SUVs were 60% of the market in 2016--but \uc2 Audi\uc2  contends there will be demand. "I think there is going to be a market," said Scott Keogh, \uc2 Audi\uc2 's top US executive, at the North American International Auto Show in Detroit. \uc2 Audi\uc2  and other manufacturers are building and planning electric cars despite low gasoline prices to deal with increasingly tough mileage and emissions regulations, and California rules mandating sales of so-called zero-emission vehicles. Electric-car demand starts with wealthy buyers--electric cars tend to be more expensive than their gas-powered counterparts--and will spread over time, especially as China and Europe embrace them more, Keogh says. Electric cars are "going to get seen, they're going to get out there and someone's going to say, 'Ah,' and then that's going to cause the tip," Keogh says. (mike.spector@wsj.com; @MikeSpectorWSJ)\par \par \uc2 10:55 ET - Chinese companies are looking for growth abroad. China's overseas direct investment reached nearly $146B in the first 10 months of 2016, up 53.3% according to official data. "Going global is almost like a buzzword now for Chinese companies," said Audrey Widjaja, an \uc2 Aon\uc2  Hewitt partner in China who helps companies there with leadership development. But Chinese companies often stumble when it comes to developing the talent to support global expansion, said Mary Teagarden, a professor at the Thunderbird School of Global Management who has helped Chinese companies craft international strategies. Firms are "still teenagers at best when it comes to those skills to go abroad," she said, noting that some companies have done poorly training and choosing employees to send overseas.(rachel.feintzeig@wsj.com; @rachelfeintzeig.)\par \par \uc2 10:50 ET - Beleaguered farmers see things getting better under President-elect Trump, following a dramatic upswing in farmer sentiment measured by \uc2 Purdue University\uc2  and futures exchange company \uc2 CME\uc2 . The benchmark reflecting farmers' future expectations jumped 51 points since October to hit an all-time high in December, Purdue says, despite weakening corn prices and only marginal improvement in soybeans during the survey period. Farmers mainly see Trump rolling back federal environmental and land-management policies viewed as restrictive in the Farm Belt. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge)\par \par \uc2 10:44 ET - \uc2 Ford\uc2  (F) is set to release several new truck and sport-utility models in the next few years, including reviving one of its most popular nameplates, the Ford Bronco. While consumers are clamoring for larger vehicles amid low-gasoline prices, the barrage of new fuel-thirsty vehicles won't do much to help \uc2 Ford\uc2 's ability to meet tougher fuel-economy regulations. The solution: more electric cars, says \uc2 Joe Hinrichs\uc2 , Ford's president of the Americas. \uc2 Ford\uc2  will also release 13 electrified vehicles by 2020, including a hybrid F-150 and Ford Mustang. "It's not a coincidence this is all happening together," Hinrichs said. "We have a battery electric SUV coming in 2020. We also have a Bronco coming in 2020." (christina.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 10, 2017 11:26 ET (16:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170110ed1a002b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi