\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Inflation Picked Up Speed in 2016\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup \par \uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2017\par 16:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY -- Mexico's inflation ended 2016 above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target as a weaker peso caused prices to accelerate in the latter part of the year, and food and energy costs also picked up.\par \par \uc2 The consumer price index rose 0.46% in December, bringing inflation for the full year to 3.36%, up from 2.13% in 2015, the National Statistics Institute said Monday. The December increase was below the 0.52% median estimate of economists polled by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Core CPI, which excludes energy and fresh fruit and vegetables, rose 0.45% in December for an annual increase of 3.44%.\par \par \uc2 Food prices, health costs, and hotels and restaurants posted the biggest increases last year. Energy costs rose in the second half of the year as oil prices recovered, but at 2.4% for the full year were below the overall rate of inflation.\par \par \uc2 After spending much of year below the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2 's 3% target, inflation accelerated in the final quarter, affected in part by an increasing impact of a weaker peso on certain goods.\par \par \uc2 The risk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation posed for inflation led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates five times last year, pushing the overnight rate target to 5.75% from 3.25% at the end of 2015, despite a sluggish economy.\par \par \uc2 More interest rate increases are expected in 2017 as inflation gathers steam from a bigger-then-usual increase in the minimum wage, continued peso losses against the U.S. dollar, and a big jump in gasoline prices at the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Banks surveyed last week by Citibanamex raised their median inflation expectation for this year to 4.7% from 4% after the government raised maximum prices for gasoline and diesel between 14% and 20% in January, preparing for the gradual elimination this year of fuel price controls. Further increases could come as oil prices continue to recover.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen of the 20 banks polled by Citibanamex expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates again in February.\par \par \uc2 The peso also stumbled at the start of the year as \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 . canceled a planned $1.6 billion assembly plant investment, stoking concerns that the incoming administration of U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could further discourage investment through protectionist measures.\par \par \uc2 The peso's slide to new lows around 21.60 to the U.S. dollar, prompted the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market, selling dollars to support the peso. The peso was trading in Mexico City around 21.28 to the dollar early Monday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anthony Harrup at anthony.harrup@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 09, 2017 10:14 ET (15:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170109ed19001x3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
