\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Stocks Fall; Peso Firms Against Dollar -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2017\par 23:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 17:09 ET - The peso gained from the previous day but posted a 2.6% loss in a volatile week that saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit new lows and then rebound thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar was quoted in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City at MXN21.2470 compared with MX21.4535 Thursday. The benchmark IPC stock index fell 1.4% to 46,072, ending a string of four straight wins. Among the few gainers were \uc2 Grupo Carso\uc2  (GCARSO.MX) and \uc2 Empresas ICA\uc2  (ICA.MX)--up 2.2% and 17%, respectively--which form part of a consortium that won a $4B contract to build the new Mexico City airport terminal. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:00 ET - Buried in the appendix of the US intelligence report on Russia's influence in the presidential election is a short nugget about the influence that RT America TV, the Kremlin-financed channel operated from within the US, has on the fracking debate in the US. The report notes that RT runs anti-fracking programming that highlights environmental issues and the public-health impacts: "This is likely reflective of the Russian Government's concern about the impact of fracking and US natural gas production on the global energy market and the potential challenges to \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 's profitability." There's long been chatter about how Russia may be promoting anti-fracking interests in the US in response to the domestic oil and gas boom of the last decade. This report gives (brief) credence to that chatter. (amy.harder@wsj.com; @AmyAHarder)\par \par \uc2 16:58 ET - Private-equity firm \uc2 Pamplona Capital Management\uc2 , which acquired \uc2 MedAssets\uc2  in January 2016 for about $2B, announces a joint venture to manage physician practices and medical groups' billing and collection efforts. The joint venture with \uc2 Northwell Health\uc2 , a hospital operator based in Great Neck, N.Y., will market consulting, scheduling and billing and collection services to physician practices, the companies say. Pamplona acquired a large healthcare billing and collection business with its \uc2 MedAssets\uc2  acquisition. \uc2 Northwell Health\uc2 , which also owns an insurance company, will be among the clients of the joint venture, called Formativ Health. (melanie.evans@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:48 ET - Hedge funds and other speculative investors added another $1.3B to bets the dollar will continue to rise in the week through Jan. 3, according to CFTC data. Net long bets, indicating a bullish outlook, stand at $26.3B in the latest week, near a 1-year-high. Many investors are wagering Trump's policies will spur economic growth and lead to higher US rates. Other positioning changes were muted, with bets against the British pound and euro edging up. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com; @chelseydulaney)\par \par \uc2 16:47 ET - Another retailer reports poor holiday sales and warns about 4Q as it cuts yearly guidance. \uc2 Stage Stores\uc2  (SSI), a specialty department store chain, says \uc2 SSS\uc2  decline 7.3% for the holiday period. The shortfall has SSI lowering 4Q EPS to 20c-35c versus its prior 65c-90c view. For the full-year, SSI now sees a loss of 70-85 cents-a-share versus the 15-40 cents-a-share yearly loss it originally projected. "Holiday comparable sales were below our expectations and increased promotions significantly pressured gross margin," CEO \uc2 Michael Glazer\uc2  says. SSI's bad news is the latest in a series of retail warnings this week that included \uc2 Macy's\uc2  (M), \uc2 Kohl's\uc2  (KSS), Victoria's Secret owner \uc2 L Brands\uc2  (LB), and others. SSI is off 12% to $3.50 after-hours. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:42 ET - With Mexico's oil production well below what it was when the government was subsidizing imported fuels and had excess oil revenue, going back on this year's gasoline price increases isn't possible despite widespread protests, Finance Minister Jose Antonio Meade tells local radio. Six years ago, for every dollar of gasoline that state oil company \uc2 Pemex\uc2  sold, it sold $2.60 worth of oil, and that has now dropped to $0.80 of oil. "The value of gasoline is now greater than that of oil production, which takes away any freedom the government has to do anything different," he adds. Regular gasoline rose 14% on Jan. 1 to about $2.86 a gallon, and premium rose 20% to $3.18. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:23 ET - Hiring in healthcare surged in December, adding 43,200 jobs across hospitals, outpatient centers, physician offices and other settings, the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reported. The December data shows a 0.3% increase in healthcare hiring last month, to bring the sector's total employment to 15.7 million jobs. Ambulatory healthcare, including outpatient centers and doctor's offices, added 29,700 jobs last month, an increase of 0.4%. Payrolls for ambulatory care in December totaled 7.3 million. Hospitals added 10,700 jobs, a gain of 0.2%, to end the year with total employment of 5.1 million. (Melanie.evans@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 16:21 ET - The US embassy in Caracas has become the latest institution to stop recognizing Venezuela's troubled bolivar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose value is in free fall. A notice on the embassy's website says all visa and consular services must now be paid in US dollars, which will mostly reduce exodus options for desperate Venezuelans who can't access greenbacks due to rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. The bolivar has lost more than 90% of its value in the last two years, as the economy crumbles while the Venezuelan government continues to print local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a frenzied pace. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com; @kejalvyas)\par \par \uc2 16:13 ET - The Canadian dollar ends the week slightly lower against the US dollar, erasing most of the gains that came after the release of strong US and Canadian December jobs reports earlier in the day. The USD is now trading around C$1.3234, from C$1.3226 late Thursday, according to CQG. The loonie grinded lower after hitting a monthly high when Canada reported a gain of 54,000 jobs in December, beating economists' expectations. \uc2 National Bank of Canada\uc2  forecasts the drivers that propelled the Canadian dollar last year, such as foreign portfolio inflows, should fade this year. "The persistence of soft oil prices and the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's dovish rhetoric could cause a further widening of US-Canada interest rate spreads," the firm says, adding USD should move toward the upper end of the C$1.30-1.40 range by midyear, before coming back down later in 2017. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:07 ET - \uc2 MD Anderson Cancer Center\uc2  underestimated how long doctors would need to get used to new electronic health records, the Houston hospital's chief financial officer said as MD Anderson announced layoffs. The hospital ended fiscal 2016 on Aug. 31 with a larger-than-expected operating loss of $266M after originally projecting a $250M operating loss for the year. "We thought we would bounce back faster," CFO Dan Fontaine said. Weak results in MD Anderson's 1Q prompted the layoffs, which total 800 to 900 workers, or about 5% of MD Anderson's workforce. To help boost revenue, MD Anderson executives urged doctors to spend more time treating patients and less time traveling to discuss research, Fontaine said. (Melanie.evans@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:06 ET - Dow Jones Industrial Average comes within 0.37 point of the 20,000 milestone, but fails to break above that level on a day where the other two major averages close at new highs. December payroll additions were slightly below expectations, while wage figures suggest higher inflation may be on the horizon. DJIA gains 64 points to 19963, the S&P 500 rises 7 to 2276 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  climbs 33 to 5521, extending its New Year's winning streak. Treasury prices pull back, with the 10-year yield rising to 2.42%, while a stronger dollar weighs on oil and gold prices. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1541 ET - \uc2 The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  fills several key positions in its leadership as it braces itself for Trump's arrival, which could bring an overhaul of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it left open one key job: Deputy Director, the No. 2 position that could become even more important during a transition period. If Director \uc2 Richard Cordray\uc2  leaves the job--either as a result of a dismissal by the new president or a voluntary resignation--whoever holds the job would take over as acting director of the agency until a new director is nominated and confirmed, a potentially lengthy process. In the absence of a permanent deputy director, that person would be David Silberman, who has served as acting deputy since January last year while overseeing the bureau's research, markets and regulations team. (yuka.hayashi@wsj.com; @TokyoWoods)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 06, 2017 17:09 ET (22:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170106ed16003ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
