\par\par \b \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  Moves to Bolster the Peso -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup \par \uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2017\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY -- The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  stepped into the exchange market Thursday for the first time in almost a year to support the peso, which hit new lows against the U.S. dollar on fears that protectionist measures by the incoming administration of U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could hurt the country's trade and investment.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commission, formed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry officials, said the dollar sales were to provide liquidity and ease the exchange volatility of recent days, and kept open the possibility of additional \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official confirmed the bank was active in the exchange market early Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The peso sank to a new all-time low against the dollar this week after \uc2 Ford Motor Co\uc2 . said it was canceling a planned $1.6 billion investment in a new assembly plant in Mexico that had been criticized by Mr. Trump. The decision led to concerns that other investments could be discouraged, limiting an important source of foreign income in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market in February 2016, when it sold $2 billion after the peso reached new lows on the decline in oil prices, which affect Mexico's trade balance and federal government revenue.\par \par \uc2 At that time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also raised interest rates and suspended its scheduled dollar auctions in favor of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The scheduled auctions, which took effect if the peso depreciated a set amount in a day, were depleting reserves without any notable benefit for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mexico's foreign reserves ended 2016 at $176.5 billion, little changed from the end of 2015. The amount of the dollar sales will be reflected in next week's reserves report.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  was justified as the peso is excessively undervalued, Goldman Sachs economist Alberto Ramos said in a note. He estimated that the real effective exchange rate -- which takes into account a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trade and inflation -- has depreciated 41% since mid-2013.\par \par \uc2 "The current peso weakness is unprecedented outside the grip of a major crisis and is also visibly weaker than the level reached during the 2008-09 global financial crisis," he said.\par \par \uc2 The peso gave back some of its initial gains and was trading in Mexico City at around 21.4365 to the dollar around 1:15 p.m. EST, according to Infosel, compared with 21.5260 at the close Wednesday. It touched an all-time low of 21.6220 on Wednesday, Banco Base said.\par \par \uc2 The peso depreciated 17% against the dollar in 2016, affected by a widening trade deficit, increasing public debt levels that prompted ratings firms to change the sovereign credit outlook to negative and the impact of lower oil prices on the finances of state oil company \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leos Mexicanos\uc2 . Mr. Trump's rise in polls and eventual election victory also put pressure on the peso.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about the impact that a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have on inflation, which accelerated toward the end of last year, prompted the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  to raise interest rates five times in 2016, increasing the overnight rate target to 5.75% from 3.25%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anthony Harrup at anthony.harrup@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 06, 2017 02:33 ET (07:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170106ed16000lj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
