\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Gives Back Some  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 -Induced Gains -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2017\par 18:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2017, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 12:49 ET - The Mexican peso has lost some of the ground made after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its support--recently trading in \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  City at 21.4330 to the US dollar vs 21.5130 late Wednesday. The Intercam brokerage says the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered the rate as far as 21.1275, which prompted dollar demand including from corporate buyers seeking to cover foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. The peso hit an all-time low 21.6220 yesterday.(anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:30 ET - Susquehanna points to a slowdown in beer depletion growth for the pressure on \uc2 Constellation Brands\uc2 ' (STZ) shares today. Despite an EPS beat, and a raised full-year earnings forecast, the brokerage says depletions--or beer sold by distributors--were below the recent pace of growth and fell shy of its 13% estimate. It also says recent scanning data for the company's flagship Corona beer show sales only up 2.8% and that it has heard that volumes in the LA metro area for Corona are down mid-single digits. It adds that "Trump risk leading to lower spending among the company's core demographic," could be a potential factor. STZ shares off 6% to $148.83. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:25 ET - GM's Cadillac brand last year eclipsed 100,000 vehicle sales in China for the first time. Sales of the luxury brand surged 46% vs 2015, to 116,406 vehicles. It's a big number considering \uc2 Cadillac\uc2  sold 170,000 in the US, where it's been selling vehicles for more than a century. China is the cornerstone of \uc2 Cadillac\uc2 's global growth plans. It has opened hundreds of dealerships in recent years and \uc2 GM\uc2  built a $1.2B assembly plant in Shanghai, opened in January 2016. \uc2 GM\uc2  is counting on a resurgent \uc2 Cadillac\uc2  to supplement its main profit engine, pickup trucks and SUVs. \uc2 Cadillac\uc2  faces stiff competition in China from entrenched luxury players like \uc2 BMW\uc2 , Mercedes and \uc2 Audi\uc2 . (mike.colias@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:08 ET - The options market reflects the obvious: gold-miner and biotech stocks are likely to be the most volatile market groups in 2017. This pair tends to see frenetic price swings and last year was no exception: the \uc2 iShares Nasdaq Biotechnology Index\uc2  (IBB) fell more than 20% last year and the VanEck Vectors Gold Miners (GDX) climbed more than 50%. Options strategists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  say the market is pricing in a 34% swing in either direction for GDX in 2017 and a 23% move in the IBB. (chris.dieterich@wsj.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 12:05 ET - The dollar's fall deepens in midday trading in New York, with the WSJ Dollar Index down 1% and on track for its biggest daily decline since September. \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 's Vassili Serebriakov says investors are unwinding big bets made on a stronger dollar under the Trump administration. "This is a realization that the market got ahead of itself," he said. To unwind those bets, investors essentially sell the dollar and buy \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Japanese yen and euro. That's helped drive the yen up about 1.5% against the dollar and the euro up 1.1%. Serebriakov says the strong-dollar trade had gotten too crowded: CFTC data from last week showed speculators holding a net $25B in bets on a rising dollar, near a one-year high. (chelsey.dulaney@wsj.com; @chelseydulaney)\par \par \uc2 12:03 ET - Wait for the TV guys. That's the hope for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (TWTR) stock, Evercore analyst Ken Sena says. After a year in which its shares declined 25%, the company offering \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  his best venting outlet is beset by weak user trends, executive departures and "waning advertiser interest" says Sena, who has a hold rating and $17 target on the shares. Why not throw in the towel? "We can appreciate where TV ad budgets may be on the brink of a broader reallocation towards digital," he writes. TWTR is down 2c to $16.84. (rolfe.winkler@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:56 ET - Third-party logistics provider \uc2 Radiant Logistics\uc2  (RLGT) is expanding its geographic and services reach with the acquisition of Lomas Logistics, the freight brokerage division of LV Lomas, the Ontario, Canada-based distributor of raw production materials. Radiant, based in Bellevue, Wash, says it signed an agreement to purchase the business through its Wheels International subsidiary. Terms of the transaction weren't disclosed. Lomas Logistics counted $13.4M in revenue in 2015, the last year in which full results were available, the companies say. Radiant shares fall 2.3% to $3.90. (paul.page@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1656 GMT - Nordic markets have a mixed session amid thin volumes, as Sweden closed at lunchtime ahead of a bank holiday in both Sweden and Finland Friday. Markets digested Wednesday night's Fed minutes which showed that FOMC members see the economic outlook as very uncertain until more information about President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's actual economic policy is disclosed. "Price actions were little affected by the FOMC Minutes last night in the US session," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says. EUR/USD inched higher while trading in Asia had a mixed session, with Japanese equity indices trading lower driven by a weaker USD, it adds. Sweden's OMXS30 index ended the day 0.6% lower, the pan-Nordic OMXN40 index flat and Oslo's oil-heavy OBX index gained 0.7%. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 ET - Oil prices reversed course following the release of the EIA's weekly inventory report, despite a 7.1M barrel drop in inventories for the week ended Dec. 30. While the decline was larger-than-expected on average by analysts and traders, a notable build in gasoline and distillate inventories outweighed the bullish sign for demand. Carl Larry, director of oil and gas at Frost & Sullivan, said a build in stocks at key location Cushing, Okla., also signaled rising domestic supply while imports dropped. Light, sweet crude futures were down 0.5% at $52.99 after trading as high as $54.12 earlier in the session. (stephanie.yang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:35 ET - One \uc2 CBOE\uc2  S&P 500 implied correlation index reached its lowest since at least November 2015, a positive sign for stock pickers at 2017 takes off. The KCJ index hit 46.58 as of yesterday, down from 60 before election day, \uc2 CBOE\uc2  data show. The index uses Dec. 2017 SPX options prices alongside prices of Jan. 2018 options on the 50 largest stocks in the S&P 500 to gauge correlation among individual stocks. Higher dispersion is generally a boon for stock pickers. (gunjan.banerji@wsj.com; @gunjanjs)\par \par \uc2 11:29 ET - Analysts warned the weekly changes in US crude oil and fuel inventories often see extraordinary, seasonal swings toward the end of the year, and they were right. Today's EIA report showed a huge, 7.1M-bbl drawdown in crude supplies, which far surpassed forecasts for a 2M-bbl decline. That big number was only outdone by fuel supplies, which saw gasoline inventories jump by 8.3M, while distillates exploded by 10.1M bbls. Investors are cautioned not too overplay these numbers given the holiday influences. But a one-week combined increase of nearly 20M bbls of gasoline and distillate supplies may be a bearish sign that's hard to ignore. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:26 ET - \uc2 Macy's\uc2  (M) decision to close 68 stores has hit mall landlords \uc2 General Growth Properties\uc2  (GGP) and \uc2 CBL\uc2  particularly hard. Both of them have five locations with closures, according to \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . M's contraction also is worrisome for investors in commercial mortgage backed securities. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  that reports 23 of the malls are encumbered by 31 CMBS loans totalling $3.4B. GGP falls 1.1% and \uc2 CBL\uc2  is down 2.8%. (peter.grant@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 05, 2017 12:49 ET (17:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020170105ed15002qw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
