\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Mexico cenbank says export diversity needed to offset US risks\b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2016\par 22:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, details from news conference, background)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 23 (Reuters) - \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday said it may revise growth estimates if new U.S. policies have a negative effect on the Mexican economy, a risk that the bank's governor said reinforced the need for Mexico to diversify its exports.\par \par \uc2 In its quarterly inflation report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tweaked its 2016 growth forecast to between 1.8-2.3 percent compared to a previous estimation of 1.7-2.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 It cut its 2017 economic growth outlook to between 1.5-2.5 percent versus a previous 2.0-3.0 percent, and said it saw 2018 growth of 2.2-3.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Right now, this is a world rich in uncertainty," Banco de Mexico Governor Agustin Carstens said at a news conference in Mexico City, adding that he thought the current volatility in markets was a temporary phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 Carstens said the risks to the U.S.-Mexico trade relationship underlined the need for Mexico to diversify its exports, and the bank said its current economic projections were based on the current commercial relationship being unchanged.\par \par \uc2 However, the bank warned upcoming growth projections could be revised if U.S. policy toward Mexico changes. Mexican exports and foreign direct investment in the country could also potentially be affected by any U.S. shifts, it added.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso was sent to a record low following the surprise election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the U.S. presidential election on Nov. 8.\par \par \uc2 During his campaign, Trump threatened to rip up the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  and build a wall along the U.S border with \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the peso after the election result, and instead waited until last week, when it hiked interest rates to their highest in over seven years.\par \par \uc2 Carstens said it was natural for the peso to weaken due to the prospect that there could be a structural change in the U.S.-Mexican relationship. He said the rate hike last week had helped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilize and noted there had not been "big" outflows from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Wednesday, Mexico's economy posted its fastest quarterly growth in more than two years, and the government stuck to its previous growth outlook, insisting there was no tangible threat yet from President-elect Trump. (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle and Gabriel Stargardter; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-23T22:20:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161123ecbn014is\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
