\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Trump plans may hurt Mexico growth, affect trade -cenbank chief\b0\par\par\uc2 468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2016\par 17:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Carstens remarks from radio interview)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 22 (Reuters) - Uncertainty over exactly what measures \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  takes as U.S. president is more likely to affect Mexican growth than tighter monetary policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Agustin Carstens said in a local newspaper interview.\par \par \uc2 In a separate radio interview on Tuesday, Carstens also said Mexico could be facing a "more lasting" change in commercial ties with the United States after Trump threatened a major shake-up in bilateral commerce during the election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Trump stirred fears about the outlook for the Mexican economy after making threats to tear up the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  and impose tariffs on Mexican-made goods. He also vowed to build a massive wall on the border to seal off illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week raised borrowing costs 50 basis points to a seven-year high of 5.25 percent in a bid to curb the risk of higher inflationary pressures sparked by a slide in the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Trump's surprise election win.\par \par \uc2 Carstens said in Tuesday's edition of paper El Financiero the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not have a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate target in mind when it raised interest rates last week.\par \par \uc2 He added he did not believe higher rates would have a major impact on growth in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 "(Tighter monetary policy) is not the main factor that may be affecting growth, above all, it's not comparable with the uncertainty being generated by the lack of precision over the measures the President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could at some point take," he told El Financiero.\par \par \uc2 In the radio interview, Carstens said pass-through from the peso's depreciation onto prices had so far been fairly gradual. However, he noted the bank would raise rates again if necessary.\par \par \uc2 On the prospect of Mexico automatically hiking rates following a \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  rate increase, Carstens said Mexico would base its decisions on the inflation outlook.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  would seek to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to support the peso, Carstens said prior \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were generally aimed at tackling temporary pressures.\par \par \uc2 "Today it's not clear we're facing a temporary shock, it's possible we're facing a change in relations between Mexico and the United States ... on trade ... that could be more lasting," he said.\par \par \uc2 Turning to threats by Trump to block remittances sent home by U.S.-based Mexican migrants to pay for the border wall, Carstens said any attempt by a \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to freeze their savings would be illegal and stir up a significant dispute. (Reporting by Dave Graham, Veronica Gomez and Frank Jack Daniel; Editing by Jeffrey Benko amd W Simon) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-22T17:40:51.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161122ecbm00p64\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
