\par\par \b \uc2 Mexico Hikes Interest Rate, What's Next For Peso? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dimitra DeFotis \par \uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2016\par 16:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico increased its policy interest rate late Thursday by 50 basis points to 5.25% and the Mexican peso was stronger by 0.5% against the U.S. dollar early Friday.\par \par \uc2 There's likely more rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come. The Mexican peso (MXN) has weakened by 15% this year, despite previous rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from "\uc2 Banxico\uc2 ." The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy face renewed weakness on fear that U.S. president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could rewrite the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 . The iShares MSCI Mexico Capped exchange-traded fund ( EWW) was down 0.5% in early trading Friday. \uc2 Coca-Cola Femsa\uc2  ( KOF) was down nearly 1%, \uc2 Grupo Financiero Santander Mexico\uc2  ( BSMX) was off by 0.3%, \uc2 Wal-Mart de Mexico\uc2 's ( WWMVY) was flat and \uc2 Cemex\uc2  ( CX) slipped 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 Benito Berber, senior Latin America strategist at \uc2 Nomura Securities\uc2  notes that the rate move was in line with some expectations but slightly below others. He expected an increase of 75 basis points, and writes:\par \par \uc2 "The board stated that it believes uncertainty and volatility in the MXN and Mexican rates markets will remain in the future, given the opaqueness with respect to future economic policies in the United States, which have not been defined by the upcoming administration ... Potential MXN depreciation in the future could materialize if Mr. Trump implements policies that negatively impact Mexico's economic outlook such as abandoning \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  [the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 ]. We believe potential MXN depreciation in the future could impact inflation expectations.\par \par \uc2 In our view, \uc2 Banxico\uc2  has relinked its own future monetary policy to that of the MXN. This could indicate that if MXN depreciates further, \uc2 Banxico\uc2  is likely to increase the policy rate to prevent an increase of the market's inflation expectations. Interestingly, we do not believe that \uc2 Banxico\uc2  signaled the start of a tightening cycle.\par \par \uc2 Remarkably, \uc2 Banxico\uc2 's communique of Thursday morning also included a line that it has used in the past with regard to the board's intention to link future changes in the policy rate depending on what actions the US Fed takes. We find this interesting because thus far this year, \uc2 Banxico\uc2  has increased its policy rate by 200 basis points, while the US Fed has kept its policy rate unchanged. The combination of these actions has obviously widened the overnight interest rate differential. Therefore, we believe \uc2 Banxico\uc2  could be indicating that the overnight interest rate spread with the US will either remain at almost 500bps, which is 100bps above the 10year average of 400bps, or possibly widen further ... we lower our expectation for a policy rate increase at the 15 December meeting to +25bps from +75bps previously. This scenario is likely to materialize should the US Fed increases its policy rate by 25bps at the 14 December meeting and also provided that Mr. Trump does not announce anything that could negatively impact the MXN before year-end, which could lead to larger rate increases."\par \par \uc2 See our posts Trump Could Dump Nafta: How To Invest In Mexico, After Peso Panic, Mexico Profits Could Surprise, Time To Buy Emerging Markets After Trump Selloff? and \uc2 Wal-Mart de Mexico\uc2 : A Top Pick Despite Mexico's Woes. \par \par 