\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-Fear rocks Mexican markets after Trump win, peso posts record slump\b0\par\par\uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2016\par 23:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds trader comments, impact of Canada statements)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 10 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso sank again on Thursday, taking its biggest two-day tumble in more than 20 years, while bonds and stocks had their worst day in five years on fears U.S. President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's anti-free trade rhetoric could hit exports.\par \par \uc2 The peso lost about 4 percent to trade at 20.64 per dollar, back near an record low hit during a major slump on Wednesday as investors punished the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Trump's unexpected victory.\par \par \uc2 During his campaign, Trump vowed to rewrite or scrap the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  (\uc2 NAFTA\uc2 ), which has helped Mexico increase its factory exports to the United States more than sixfold since 1994 to $316 billion a year.\par \par \uc2 During the last decade, the peso, as the most liquid emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been a proxy bet for all emerging markets and risky investments like stocks, but is now caught in a downward spiral due to Mexico's U.S. links.\par \par \uc2 "Is this a paradigm shift? I'm scared," said Alfredo Puig, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Vector in Monterrey, Mexico. "I look at the stock markets, and there is no panic in the world. But in the peso, there is panic."\par \par \uc2 The peso diverged from rising U.S. stocks for the second day in a row. After its two-day rout, the peso has become the worst performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, displacing the British pound.\par \par \uc2 Losses deepened after Canadian officials said the country could fall back on a free-trade agreement that excludes Mexico if Trump follows through on his threats.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has risen about 12.6 percent against the peso in the last two days, its biggest gain since a Mexican 1994-1995 devaluation.\par \par \uc2 The peso is on track to post its worst year since 2008. The dollar has strengthened about 19.6 percent against the peso, even more than a nearly 17 percent gain for all of 2015.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's IPC stock index sank 4.6 percent in its biggest one day-drop in five years, closing at its lowest since late June. Auto parts maker and exporter Nemak lost more than 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  strategist Dirk Willer said he expected the peso, long a bellwether for investor sentiment about the U.S. election, to keep weakening and range between 21 and 22 per dollar until it is clear what trade policies Trump will enact.\par \par \uc2 "It will be very hard for the peso to rally until you get some clarity on whether Trump really means business," Willer said.\par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the election, a Reuters poll had forecast a Trump victory would knock the peso to around 21 per dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem peso losses the morning after Trump's victory, a move many analysts had expected, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise interest rates next week for the fourth time this year.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the country's benchmark 10-year peso bond shot up 48 basis points to bid at 7.07 percent, its highest since mid-2011. Yield on the 10-year bond has spiked 91 basis points in the last two days, its biggest two-day jump since the crash of October 2008.\par \par \uc2 In recent years foreign investors significantly increased their holdings of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt to 2.014 trillion pesos ($98 billion) as of Oct. 31, according to the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.\par \par \uc2 If foreign investors begin to dump those bonds, it could accelerate the peso's losses as they buy dollars instead.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's Finance Ministry\uc2  said it would cut the amount of long-term peso bonds it would offer during the rest of the fourth quarter due to the increase in market volatility.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 20.4600 Mexican pesos) (Reporting by Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Andrew Hay and Lisa Shumaker) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-10T23:18:31.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161110ecba00qmi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
