\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Plunges, Yen Soars in Jittery Asian Trade -- 3rd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2016\par 09:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--Central bankers and traders around the globe scrambled to deal with soaring and plunging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after polling results in the U.S. presidential election showed Republican candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  clinching a surprise victory.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, where the yen spiked as much as 3.9% against the dollar Wednesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance officials convened an emergency meeting while the government's chief spokesman warned it will take "appropriate action" if the volatility continued. The Korean won slumped 1.5% against the dollar in Asian trading, and some traders said they suspect that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to help tame volatility. \uc2 The Bank of Korea\uc2  declined to comment on whether it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso--seen as a loser in a Trump presidency--plunged 7% against the dollar. Other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also sold off sharply as Mr. Trump neared victory. The U.S. dollar hit an all-time high against the Turkish lira and the Mongolian togrog.\par \par \uc2 The whirlwind in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a result of a stunning flip in expectations on who the next president of the U.S. will be. Investors and traders saw Democrat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  as the favored winner as U.S. voting wound down Tuesday evening. But when exit polls and early results showed Trump wins in key states, trades quickly reversed.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Clinton conceded the election in the wee hours of Wednesday morning in New York.\par \par \uc2 "When we came in, in Asia time, the market was telling us this was a done deal for Clinton," said Kay Van-Petersen, global macro strategist at Saxo Capital Markets in Singapore. "You're seeing that unravel," he said. Mr. Van-Petersen said all the TVs were on in his office and he was watching several websites for the latest updates on election results.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen, a favorite place to stash cash in times of turmoil, notched one of its biggest intraday moves since the day the U.K. voted to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Yen strength erodes profits at global manufacturers like \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 ., which are the backbone of the country's attempts to spur growth. Japan's benchmark Nikkei stock index fell 5.4% to 16,251.54 on Wednesday, the biggest one-day drop since Brexit.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary, \uc2 Yoshihide Suga\uc2 , said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock movements have been "speculative" and "one-sided" and warned that such swings could have "extremely negative effects on the stability of the economy and the financial system."\par \par \uc2 "If such movements continue, the government will take appropriate measures," he said.\par \par \uc2 One U.S. dollar bought 103.42 yen recently, compared with Yen 105.14 in late New York trading Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in Asian trading, the Mexican peso's fall put the U.S. dollar at its highest level on record against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , based on \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  data going back to 1989. The peso is sensitive to the outcome of the U.S. election, as Mr. Trump's vows to clamp down on immigration and impose high tariffs on major U.S. trading partners are likely to hurt Mexico's economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's win is likely to further bolster safe-haven assets like the yen and gold, investors and traders say, as it is unclear how his campaign pledges to be tougher on many trade partners and crack down on immigration would pan out.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose against the Korean won and Taiwan dollar. Both Taiwan and Korea are major exporters that could be hit by any policies that restrict global trade, such as the tariffs promised by Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Wong, head of emerging markets trading for Asia Pacific, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 , said the euro, Swiss franc and yen were among the most traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on her desk as the U.S. election results streamed in, while the Mexican peso and Turkish lira were more active than normal in Asian trading hours. "Real-money investors are not really in today," she said, adding that trading volumes in several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , excluding the yen, were lower than normal.\par \par \uc2 Suryatapa Bhattacharya and Mitsuru Obe in Tokyo and Gregor Stuart Hunter in Hong Kong contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 09, 2016 03:40 ET (08:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161109ecb9001hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
