\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Strengthens Against Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2016\par 00:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY\u8212\'20\'14The Mexican peso, one of the world's most actively traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , strengthened 1.3% against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday, extending gains for the fourth consecutive session as Democratic nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  consolidated her lead against her Republican opponent in recent days.\par \par \uc2 The peso closed at 18.38 to the dollar in Mexico City, compared with 18.6485 Monday, a two-month high and far from the record low of almost 20 pesos to the dollar it hit in late September. Local equity prices also jumped ahead of U.S. election results, with the benchmark IPC index rising 0.9% to 48,470 points, its highest level in three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Mexican assets took a roller-coaster ride in recent months, as the peso became a key emerging market proxy for the U.S. presidential election. Each time Republican nominee \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gained in polls, the peso took a beating against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's broad gains "reflected optimism over hopes that Mrs. Clinton would get to the White House," said Gabriela Siller, chief economist at local brokerage Banco Base.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's pledge to tear up the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2  generated anxiety in a country that sends nearly 80% of its exports to the U.S. Mr. Trump has also pledged to crack down on immigration and to build a wall in the U.S.-Mexico border\u8212\'20\'14and make Mexico pay for it.\par \par \uc2 "If Mr. Trump wins, it would be a Category 5 hurricane for the Mexican economy," Bank of Mexico Governor Agust\u237\'00\'ED n Carstens said in a recent TV interview.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the peso to further strengthen in coming days and puncture through key resistance levels if Mrs. Clinton wins the presidency. Nomura says the peso could reach 17.50 to the dollar next month under benign market conditions.\par \par \uc2 The peso is the 10th most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and frequently used as a proxy to hedge global risks, according to the International Bank of Settlements. An average volume of around US$112 billion is traded daily. It is also favored by investors because it trades globally around the clock, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u180\'00\'B4 s orthodox \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rarely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 In the past year, it has suffered multiple selloffs because of its liquidity and global prominence, from Britain's vote in June to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  to increasing expectations the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates this year and, more recently, uncertainty surrounding the U.S. presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Write to Juan Montes at juan.montes@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 08, 2016 18:35 ET (23:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161108ecb8004t7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
