\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Mexico prepares contingency plan for U.S. election -Carstens\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2016\par 18:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, comment from finance minister, analysts)\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, Nov 3 (Reuters) - Mexico is readying a contingency plan for an "adverse" result in the U.S. presidential election, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Agustin Carstens, who has previously stated that a victory for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would hit his country like a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 "If the adverse situation materializes it's foreseeable that the Mexican authorities respond in some way," Carstens told Milenio television late on Wednesday. "It's a contingency plan that we're discussing with the Finance Ministry. We hope we don't have to use it."\par \par \uc2 Without naming Republican nominee Trump, Carstens said there was an "adverse" candidate for Mexico in Tuesday's U.S. election, which is due to be held on Nov. 8. He also noted there could be market volatility following the vote, regardless of whether Trump or Democrat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  wins.\par \par \uc2 "Either way, we the authorities would need to adjust our policy position if it's necessary," he said.\par \par \uc2 Carstens did not provide details of the contingency plan.\par \par \uc2 Trump's threats to tear up a trade deal with Mexico, build a massive border wall and his attacks on U.S. companies investing there have spooked investors, hurting the peso.\par \par \uc2 Interest rate increases and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market have been \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main tools to support the peso.\par \par \uc2 Senior officials from the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  as well as the Finance and Economy ministries were holding discussions on Wednesday about U.S. election scenarios, an official said ahead of the talks, speaking on condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 Economists at 4Cast said on Thursday they believed the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  will attempt to smooth the transition to a Trump presidency using a combination of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and rate hikes, possibly to the tune of 150 basis points.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Finance Minister Jose Antonio Meade said when volatility is caused by global concerns, such as jitters over the U.S. election outcome, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market would be like "pouring drops of water into the ocean."\par \par \uc2 "It is not an instrument that would work," he said.\par \par \uc2 Carstens has said monetary policy could change depending on the U.S. election result, noting that a favorable outcome for Mexico might obviate the need for the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  to follow any impending U.S. rate hike.\par \par \uc2 On Sept. 30 Carstens likened a Trump win to a hurricane hitting Mexico, and said a Clinton victory would be better for the economy.\par \par \uc2 Maintaining Mexican inflation close to its target rate of 3 percent would be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "guiding principle" going forward, Carstens said. (Reporting by Dave Graham and Adriana Barrera; Editing by Alistair Bell and Bill Trott) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-3T18:18:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161103ecb300vn4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
