\par\par \b \uc2 Mexican Peso Hit by Risks From Brexit to Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2016\par 21:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY -- Mexico's peso slid Tuesday to its weakest level against the U.S. dollar since February, moving toward the record lows that prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year to raise interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The prospects of higher U.S. interest rates in coming months, concerns that the U.K. will vote next week to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and the possibility that Donald Trump could win the U.S. presidential election in November have all put downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past month.\par \par \uc2 The peso weakened for a fourth consecutive session Tuesday, briefly moving above 19 to the dollar and trading in Mexico City around 18.97 in early afternoon. The \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market since Feb. 17, when it sold $2 billion just days after the peso had fallen to its weakest ever at 19.4485 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also raised interest rates at the time by a half percentage point following an unscheduled meeting.\par \par \uc2 "With USD/MXN making new highs for this move, the February all-time high near 19.45 looms closer," \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  said in a note. "We downplay any imminent action to support the peso, but acknowledge that the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  may hike rates at the June 30 meeting if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness persists."\par \par \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  Gov. Agust\u237\'00\'EDn Carstens said at a May 25 press conference that the bank hasn't noted the kind of automated trading against the peso that led to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February, and that there was no evidence of a speculative attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There have been fundamental reasons that explain that depreciation," he said.\par \par \uc2 The peso has since continued to slide, however, despite a recovery in world oil prices that is positive for government revenue and for cash-strapped state oil company \uc2 Petr\u243\'00\'F3leos Mexicanos\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Recent polls showing the British leaning toward a vote to leave Europe have added to the pressure. Even though a U.K. exit from the \uc2 EU\uc2  is unlikely to have much direct impact on Mexico's economy, it could hurt the peso by tightening global financial conditions and increasing global risk aversion, said Nomura Latin America strategist Benito Berber.\par \par \uc2 A leave vote on June 23 could push the peso, one of the world's most-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to a new all-time low at 19.50 to the dollar and prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Gabriela Siller, head of research at \b \highlight22\uc2 Mexico\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Banco Base.\par \par \uc2 Analysts note that the peso depreciation accelerated when Mr. Trump virtually clinched the Republican presidential candidacy given his plans to build a wall along the U.S.-Mexican border and claims he will make Mexico pay for it by threatening to curb remittances from Mexicans in the U.S., impose trade tariffs or restrict visas.\par \par \uc2 "The peso is perhaps one of the most vulnerable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the impact of the policies proposed by Mr. Trump," Mr. Berber said.\par \par \uc2 In anticipation of further risks ahead, the Mexican government in May obtained a new two-year flexible credit line with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , extending the amount to $88 billion from $67 billion. The government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cited risks including low oil prices, slowing global growth and lower trade and capital flows, as well as uncertainty over the pace at which the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely seen leaving interest rates unchanged Wednesday following a weak May employment report, but could fuel risk aversion by leaving increases on the table for July.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  is expected to follow any Fed interest-rate increases by raising the local rate as it did in December, although several analysts think the bank could move before the Fed if the peso continues to sell off.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anthony Harrup at anthony.harrup@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 14, 2016 15:21 ET (19:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160614ec6e0034v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
