\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  gain, Mexico rules out imminent hike\b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Mai 2016\par 23:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MEXICO CITY, May 26 (Reuters) - Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strengthened on Thursday after weak U.S. data on business\par spending plans dampened expectations for a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par interest rate increase in the coming months.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Commerce Department\f28 \uc2 \f2  data showed non-defense capital\par goods orders excluding aircraft, a closely watched proxy for\par business spending plans, fell for the third month in a row in\par April. \par     Riskier assets like emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have fallen \par in May on expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise\par interest rates in June or July which could push investors to\par sell emerging market debt and buy U.S. Treasuries.\par     Mexico's peso has shed nearly 7.0 percent in May, the second\par worst performer among the 36-most traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , behind the\par South African rand. The peso's tumble stoked expectations that\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets or raise interest\par rates next month in a bid to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Mexico \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agustin Carstens\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Thursday\par said he saw no current need for an extraordinary rate-setting\par meeting. On Wednesday, Carstens said he saw no sign of a\par speculative attack on the peso. \par     A sharp drop in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Mexico\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's peso pushed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par February to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  directly in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first\par time since 2009 and to raise interest rates unexpectedly after\par an unannounced meeting.\par     The peso closed up 0.24 percent. \par     The IPC stock index closed the day up 0.72 percent, with\par shares in retailer Walmex, up 1.97 percent, driving\par gains. Shares in breadmaker Bimbo fell 1.85 percent.\par     Brazilian markets were closed on Thursday for Corpus Christi\par day. \par     The Chilean peso rose 0.54 percent, helped by a\par rise in the price of copper, the country's main export. \par     \par     Key Latin American stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 21:43\par GMT:\par     \par  Stock indexes                  Latest    Daily pct   YTD pct\par                                            change     change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 MSCI\f28 \uc2 \f2  Emerging Markets          803.74      0.51        0.7\par                                                      \par  MSCI LatAm                    2,077.27     0.45       13.01\par  Brazil Bovespa                49,482.86    0.28       14.15\par  Mexico IPC                    46,039.69    0.72       7.12\par  Chile IPSA                    3,952.18     -0.16      7.39\par  Chile IGPA                    19,505.54    -0.1       7.46\par  Argentina MerVal              12,727.75    2.79       9.02\par  Colombia IGBC                 9,684.84     -0.24      13.31\par  Venezuela IBC                 15,350.84    0.24       5.23\par                          